f"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"Strongest acid","a":"Which of these is the strongest acid in DMSO?","o":[{"m":199,"x":"Benzoxazolone","v":"pKa 12.1"},{"m":174,"x":"Formamide","v":"pKa 23.5"},{"m":169,"x":"Hexamethyldisilazane","v":"pKa 30"},{"m":167,"x":"Pyrrolidine","v":"pKa 44"}],"ex":"pKa in DMSO: Benzoxazolone 12.1 < Formamide 23.5 < Hexamethyldisilazane 30 < Pyrrolidine 44. The lowest pKa belongs to the strongest acid.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"Strongest acid","a":"Which of these is the strongest acid in DMSO?","o":[{"m":186,"x":"Benzenesulfonamide","v":"pKa 16.1"},{"m":182,"x":"4-Benzyl-2-oxazolidinone","v":"pKa 20.5"},{"m":177,"x":"Urea","v":"pKa 26.9"},{"m":167,"x":"Pyrrolidine","v":"pKa 44"}],"ex":"pKa in DMSO: Benzenesulfonamide 16.1 < 4-Benzyl-2-oxazolidinone 20.5 < Urea 26.9 < Pyrrolidine 44. The lowest pKa belongs to the strongest acid.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"Strongest acid","a":"Which of these is the strongest acid in DMSO?","o":[{"m":173,"x":"Hydrazoic acid","v":"pKa 7.9"},{"m":197,"x":"4-Pyridone","v":"pKa 14.8"},{"m":180,"x":"2-Piperidinone","v":"pKa 26.4"},{"m":170,"x":"Aniline","v":"pKa 30.6"}],"ex":"pKa in DMSO: Hydrazoic acid 7.9 < 4-Pyridone 14.8 < 2-Piperidinone 26.4 < Aniline 30.6. The lowest pKa belongs to the strongest acid.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"Strongest acid","a":"Which of these is the strongest acid in DMSO?","o":[{"m":187,"x":"Triflamide","v":"pKa 9.7"},{"m":193,"x":"Benzimidazole","v":"pKa 16.4"},{"m":189,"x":"Pyrrole","v":"pKa 23"},{"m":168,"x":"2,2,6,6-Tetramethylpiperidine","v":"pKa 37"}],"ex":"pKa in DMSO: Triflamide 9.7 < Benzimidazole 16.4 < Pyrrole 23 < 2,2,6,6-Tetramethylpiperidine 37. The lowest pKa belongs to the strongest acid.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"Strongest acid","a":"Which of these is the strongest acid in DMSO?","o":[{"m":172,"x":"Cyanamide","v":"pKa 16.9"},{"m":189,"x":"Pyrrole","v":"pKa 23"},{"m":46,"x":"Ammonia","v":"pKa 41"},{"m":167,"x":"Pyrrolidine","v":"pKa 44"}],"ex":"pKa in DMSO: Cyanamide 16.9 < Pyrrole 23 < Ammonia 41 < Pyrrolidine 44. The lowest pKa belongs to the strongest acid.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"Strongest acid","a":"Which of these is the strongest acid in DMSO?","o":[{"m":195,"x":"Benzotriazole","v":"pKa 11.9"},{"m":172,"x":"Cyanamide","v":"pKa 16.9"},{"m":174,"x":"Formamide","v":"pKa 23.5"},{"m":169,"x":"Hexamethyldisilazane","v":"pKa 30"}],"ex":"pKa in DMSO: Benzotriazole 11.9 < Cyanamide 16.9 < Formamide 23.5 < Hexamethyldisilazane 30. The lowest pKa belongs to the strongest acid.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Isobutane, in water?","o":[{"x":"53"},{"x":"36","w":"NaH, KH"},{"x":"5.21","w":"Pyridine"},{"x":"10.75","w":"Et₃N"}],"t":"t-BuLi","f":"(CH₃)₃CLi","fig":{"m":200},"ex":"Isobutane, the conjugate acid of t-BuLi, has pKa 53 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Extrapolated value.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Butane, in water?","o":[{"x":"50"},{"x":"15.5","w":"NaOMe"},{"x":"12.32","w":"K₃PO₄"},{"x":"6.4","w":"NaHCO₃"}],"t":"n-BuLi","f":"CH₃(CH₂)₃Li","fig":{"m":201},"ex":"Butane, the conjugate acid of n-BuLi, has pKa 50 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Butane is not in the Evans table; 50 is the usual textbook figure.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Methane, in water?","o":[{"x":"48"},{"x":"38","w":"NaNH₂"},{"x":"8.82","w":"DABCO"},{"x":"6.4","w":"NaHCO₃"}],"t":"MeLi","f":"CH₃Li","fig":{"m":50},"ex":"Methane, the conjugate acid of MeLi, has pKa 48 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Also 56 in DMSO. Extrapolated value.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Benzene, in water?","o":[{"x":"43"},{"x":"17","w":"KOt-Bu"},{"x":"50","w":"n-BuLi"},{"x":"7.38","w":"N-Methylmorpholine"}],"t":"PhLi","f":"C₆H₅Li","fig":{"m":48},"ex":"Benzene, the conjugate acid of PhLi, has pKa 43 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Ammonia, in water?","o":[{"x":"38"},{"x":"7.38","w":"N-Methylmorpholine"},{"x":"50","w":"n-BuLi"},{"x":"53","w":"t-BuLi"}],"t":"NaNH₂","f":"NaNH₂","fig":{"m":46},"ex":"Ammonia, the conjugate acid of NaNH₂, has pKa 38 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Also 41 in DMSO.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Hydrogen, in water?","o":[{"x":"36"},{"x":"43","w":"PhLi"},{"x":"7.38","w":"N-Methylmorpholine"},{"x":"53","w":"t-BuLi"}],"t":"NaH, KH","f":"NaH","fig":{"m":44},"ex":"Hydrogen, the conjugate acid of NaH, KH, has pKa 36 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Approximate. Insoluble, so it reacts at the solid surface.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, tert-Butanol, in water?","o":[{"x":"17"},{"x":"4.76","w":"NaOAc"},{"x":"43","w":"PhLi"},{"x":"36","w":"NaH, KH"}],"t":"KOt-Bu","f":"KOC(CH₃)₃","fig":{"m":38},"ex":"tert-Butanol, the conjugate acid of KOt-Bu, has pKa 17 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Also 29.4 in DMSO. In DMSO it is a far stronger base.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Methanol, in water?","o":[{"x":"15.5"},{"x":"12","w":"Proton sponge"},{"x":"6.75","w":"2,6-Lutidine"},{"x":"36","w":"NaH, KH"}],"t":"NaOMe","f":"NaOCH₃","fig":{"m":36},"ex":"Methanol, the conjugate acid of NaOMe, has pKa 15.5 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Also 27.9 in DMSO.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Water, in water?","o":[{"x":"14"},{"x":"38","w":"NaNH₂"},{"x":"6.4","w":"NaHCO₃"},{"x":"53","w":"t-BuLi"}],"t":"NaOH, KOH","f":"NaOH","fig":{"m":34},"ex":"Water, the conjugate acid of NaOH, KOH, has pKa 14 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. The 2024 Evans table gives 14.0. Older books use 15.7.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Hydrogen phosphate, in water?","o":[{"x":"12.32"},{"x":"53","w":"t-BuLi"},{"x":"4.95","w":"2,6-Di-tert-butylpyridine"},{"x":"50","w":"n-BuLi"}],"t":"K₃PO₄","f":"K₃PO₄","fig":{"m":202},"ex":"Hydrogen phosphate, the conjugate acid of K₃PO₄, has pKa 12.32 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Third pKa of phosphoric acid.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Proton sponge-H⁺, in water?","o":[{"x":"12"},{"x":"15.5","w":"NaOMe"},{"x":"6.95","w":"Imidazole"},{"x":"9.2","w":"DMAP"}],"t":"Proton sponge","fig":{"m":161},"ex":"Proton sponge-H⁺, the conjugate acid of Proton sponge, has pKa 12 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Strongly basic but barely nucleophilic.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Quinuclidinium, in water?","o":[{"x":"11"},{"x":"6.95","w":"Imidazole"},{"x":"15.5","w":"NaOMe"},{"x":"48","w":"MeLi"}],"t":"Quinuclidine","fig":{"m":150},"ex":"Quinuclidinium, the conjugate acid of Quinuclidine, has pKa 11 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Triethylammonium, in water?","o":[{"x":"10.75"},{"x":"4.95","w":"2,6-Di-tert-butylpyridine"},{"x":"53","w":"t-BuLi"},{"x":"6.95","w":"Imidazole"}],"t":"Et₃N","f":"(CH₃CH₂)₃N","fig":{"m":30},"ex":"Triethylammonium, the conjugate acid of Et₃N, has pKa 10.75 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Also 9 in DMSO.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Bicarbonate, in water?","o":[{"x":"10.3"},{"x":"7.38","w":"N-Methylmorpholine"},{"x":"17","w":"KOt-Bu"},{"x":"48","w":"MeLi"}],"t":"K₂CO₃, Cs₂CO₃","f":"K₂CO₃","fig":{"m":27},"ex":"Bicarbonate, the conjugate acid of K₂CO₃, Cs₂CO₃, has pKa 10.3 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Textbook value: carbonate is not in the Evans table.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, DMAP-H⁺, in water?","o":[{"x":"9.2"},{"x":"12.32","w":"K₃PO₄"},{"x":"50","w":"n-BuLi"},{"x":"15.5","w":"NaOMe"}],"t":"DMAP","fig":{"m":165},"ex":"DMAP-H⁺, the conjugate acid of DMAP, has pKa 9.2 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Mostly used as a nucleophilic acylation catalyst.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, DABCO-H⁺, in water?","o":[{"x":"8.82"},{"x":"38","w":"NaNH₂"},{"x":"4.95","w":"2,6-Di-tert-butylpyridine"},{"x":"12","w":"Proton sponge"}],"t":"DABCO","fig":{"m":153},"ex":"DABCO-H⁺, the conjugate acid of DABCO, has pKa 8.82 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, N-Methylmorpholinium, in water?","o":[{"x":"7.38"},{"x":"5.21","w":"Pyridine"},{"x":"48","w":"MeLi"},{"x":"10.75","w":"Et₃N"}],"t":"N-Methylmorpholine","f":"NMM","fig":{"m":152},"ex":"N-Methylmorpholinium, the conjugate acid of N-Methylmorpholine, has pKa 7.38 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Imidazolium, in water?","o":[{"x":"6.95"},{"x":"4.95","w":"2,6-Di-tert-butylpyridine"},{"x":"50","w":"n-BuLi"},{"x":"10.75","w":"Et₃N"}],"t":"Imidazole","fig":{"m":19},"ex":"Imidazolium, the conjugate acid of Imidazole, has pKa 6.95 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Used with TBSCl to make silyl ethers.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, 2,6-Lutidinium, in water?","o":[{"x":"6.75"},{"x":"9.2","w":"DMAP"},{"x":"14","w":"NaOH, KOH"},{"x":"36","w":"NaH, KH"}],"t":"2,6-Lutidine","fig":{"m":162},"ex":"2,6-Lutidinium, the conjugate acid of 2,6-Lutidine, has pKa 6.75 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Hindered, so a poor nucleophile. Often paired with TBSOTf.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Carbonic acid, in water?","o":[{"x":"6.4"},{"x":"17","w":"KOt-Bu"},{"x":"43","w":"PhLi"},{"x":"12","w":"Proton sponge"}],"t":"NaHCO₃","f":"NaHCO₃","fig":{"m":18},"ex":"Carbonic acid, the conjugate acid of NaHCO₃, has pKa 6.4 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Textbook value.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Pyridinium, in water?","o":[{"x":"5.21"},{"x":"12","w":"Proton sponge"},{"x":"38","w":"NaNH₂"},{"x":"48","w":"MeLi"}],"t":"Pyridine","f":"C₅H₅N","fig":{"m":17},"ex":"Pyridinium, the conjugate acid of Pyridine, has pKa 5.21 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Also 3.4 in DMSO.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, 2,6-Di-tert-butylpyridinium, in water?","o":[{"x":"4.95"},{"x":"9.2","w":"DMAP"},{"x":"12.32","w":"K₃PO₄"},{"x":"43","w":"PhLi"}],"t":"2,6-Di-tert-butylpyridine","fig":{"m":163},"ex":"2,6-Di-tert-butylpyridinium, the conjugate acid of 2,6-Di-tert-butylpyridine, has pKa 4.95 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. So hindered that it takes protons and little else.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"pKa of a base","a":"What is the pKa of its conjugate acid, Acetic acid, in water?","o":[{"x":"4.76"},{"x":"53","w":"t-BuLi"},{"x":"43","w":"PhLi"},{"x":"10.75","w":"Et₃N"}],"t":"NaOAc","f":"CH₃CO₂Na","fig":{"m":16},"ex":"Acetic acid, the conjugate acid of NaOAc, has pKa 4.76 in water: the higher this pKa, the stronger the base. Also 12.3 in DMSO.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Evans pKa table (Ripin & Evans, ACS Organic Division, 2024)","https://organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/evans_pKa_table.pdf"]]}]},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"carboxylic acid O–H (pKa 3.86)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"alcohol O–H, as in ethanol (pKa ≈16)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"alkyl C–H, as in an alkane (pKa ≈50)"}],"t":"Lactic acid","f":"CH₃CH(OH)CO₂H","fig":{"sites":203},"ex":"A carboxylate shares its charge between two oxygens; an alkoxide keeps it on one."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"alcohol O–H (pKa 13.6)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"alkyne C–H, as in acetylene (pKa ≈25)"}],"t":"Propargyl alcohol","f":"HC≡CCH₂OH","fig":{"sites":204},"ex":"Oxygen carries a negative charge far better than carbon, even an sp carbon."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"C–H","x":"alkyne C–H, as in acetylene (pKa ≈25)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"amine N–H, as in ammonia (pKa ≈38)"}],"t":"Propargylamine","f":"HC≡CCH₂NH₂","fig":{"sites":205},"ex":"Here the C–H wins: an acetylide is more stable than an amide anion R–NH⁻. That is why NaNH₂ deprotonates terminal alkynes."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"alcohol O–H, as in ethanol (pKa ≈16)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"amine N–H, as in ammonia (pKa ≈38)"}],"t":"Ethanolamine","f":"HOCH₂CH₂NH₂","fig":{"sites":206},"ex":"Oxygen is more electronegative than nitrogen. The amine is the basic site of this molecule, not the acidic one."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"N–H","x":"ammonium N–H (pKa 9.5)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"alcohol O–H, as in ethanol (pKa ≈16)"}],"t":"Ethanolammonium","f":"HOCH₂CH₂NH₃⁺","fig":{"sites":207},"ex":"Once the amine is protonated, its N–H is the acidic proton: losing it gives a neutral amine, not an anion."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"S–H","x":"thiol S–H (pKa 9.6)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"alcohol O–H, as in ethanol (pKa ≈16)"}],"t":"2-Mercaptoethanol","f":"HSCH₂CH₂OH","fig":{"sites":208},"ex":"The S–H bond is weaker than O–H and the large sulfur atom spreads the charge, so a thiol is about a million times more acidic than an alcohol."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"phenol O–H (pKa 9.8)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"alcohol O–H, as in benzyl alcohol (pKa ≈15.4)"}],"t":"4-Hydroxybenzyl alcohol","f":"HOC₆H₄CH₂OH","fig":{"sites":209},"ex":"The phenoxide charge is delocalised into the ring. The CH₂ cuts the alkoxide off from it."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"carboxylic acid O–H (pKa 4.54)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"phenol O–H (pKa 9.3)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"aryl C–H, as in benzene (pKa ≈43)"}],"t":"4-Hydroxybenzoic acid","f":"HOC₆H₄CO₂H","fig":{"sites":210},"ex":"Both O–H are acidic, but the carboxylate shares its charge between two equivalent oxygens."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"phenol O–H (pKa 8)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"α C–H, as in acetone (pKa ≈19)"}],"t":"4-Hydroxyacetophenone","f":"HOC₆H₄COCH₃","fig":{"sites":211},"ex":"The para acetyl group takes the phenoxide charge onto its oxygen, so this phenol is stronger than phenol itself (9.95)."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in DMSO?","o":[{"pre":"N–H","x":"amide N–H (pKa 25.5)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"α C–H, as in N,N-dimethylacetamide (pKa ≈35)"}],"t":"Acetamide","f":"CH₃CONH₂","fig":{"sites":212},"ex":"The N–H anion is shared between N and O. The α C–H of an amide is the least acidic of all carbonyl α-protons."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in DMSO?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"phenol O–H, as in phenol (pKa ≈18)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"aniline N–H, as in aniline (pKa ≈31)"}],"t":"4-Aminophenol","f":"H₂NC₆H₄OH","fig":{"sites":213},"ex":"Both anions are delocalised into the ring, so electronegativity decides: O beats N."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"C–H","x":"C–H between the two C=O (pKa 9)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"C–H next to one C=O, as in acetone (pKa ≈19)"}],"t":"Acetylacetone","f":"CH₃COCH₂COCH₃","fig":{"sites":214},"ex":"The CH₂ between two carbonyls gives an enolate shared by both oxygens."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"C–H","x":"C–H between the ketone and the ester (pKa 11)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"C–H next to the ketone only, as in acetone (pKa ≈19)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"O–CH₂ C–H, no stabilising group (pKa ≈50)"}],"t":"Ethyl acetoacetate","f":"CH₃COCH₂CO₂Et","fig":{"sites":215},"ex":"The doubly activated CH₂ is the carbon alkylated in the acetoacetic ester synthesis."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"C–H","x":"C–H between the two C=O (pKa 5.26)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"C–H next to one C=O, as in acetone (pKa ≈19)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"C–H next to no C=O, as in an alkane (pKa ≈50)"}],"t":"1,3-Cyclohexanedione","fig":{"sites":216},"ex":"Two carbonyls bring this C–H down to the acidity of acetic acid."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in DMSO?","o":[{"pre":"C–H","x":"benzylic C–H next to the C=O (pKa 19.8)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"methyl C–H next to the C=O, as in acetone (pKa ≈26.5)"}],"t":"Phenylacetone","f":"C₆H₅CH₂COCH₃","fig":{"sites":217},"ex":"Both are α to the ketone, but the benzylic enolate is also conjugated with the ring."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic on the water scale?","o":[{"pre":"C–H","x":"C–H next to the ketone, as in acetone (pKa ≈19)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"C–H next to the ester, as in ethyl acetate (pKa ≈25)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"O–CH₃ C–H, no stabilising group (pKa ≈50)"}],"t":"Methyl levulinate","f":"CH₃COCH₂CH₂CO₂CH₃","fig":{"sites":218},"ex":"A ketone is more acidic at its α-carbon than an ester: the ester's OR group already donates into its C=O."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in DMSO?","o":[{"pre":"C–H","x":"sp³ C–H of the CH₂ (pKa 18)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"vinyl C–H, as in ethylene (pKa ≈44)"}],"t":"Cyclopentadiene","f":"C₅H₆","fig":{"sites":219},"ex":"Removing a CH₂ proton gives the cyclopentadienyl anion, which is aromatic (6 π electrons)."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in DMSO?","o":[{"pre":"C–H","x":"benzylic C–H (pKa 20.4)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"aryl C–H, as in benzene (pKa ≈43)"}],"t":"p-Nitrotoluene","f":"O₂NC₆H₄CH₃","fig":{"sites":220},"ex":"The nitro group takes the benzylic charge by resonance. Toluene itself is 43 in DMSO."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in DMSO?","o":[{"pre":"C–H","x":"alkyne C–H (sp) (pKa 28.8)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"aryl C–H (sp²), as in benzene (pKa ≈43)"}],"t":"Phenylacetylene","f":"C₆H₅C≡CH","fig":{"sites":221},"ex":"More s character holds the anion's lone pair closer to the nucleus: sp beats sp²."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in water?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"carboxylic acid O–H (pKa 2.34)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"ammonium N–H (pKa 9.6)"}],"t":"Glycine, fully protonated","f":"H₃N⁺CH₂CO₂H","fig":{"sites":222},"ex":"The nearby NH₃⁺ makes this CO₂H much stronger than acetic acid (4.76). At pH 7 glycine is a zwitterion."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in water?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"α-CO₂H, next to the NH₃⁺ (pKa 2.19)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"side-chain CO₂H (pKa 4.25)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"ammonium N–H (pKa 9.67)"}],"t":"Glutamic acid, fully protonated","fig":{"sites":223},"ex":"Both are carboxylic acids, but the α one sits next to the positive NH₃⁺, which stabilises its carboxylate."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in water?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"carboxylic acid O–H (pKa 1.82)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"imidazolium N–H (pKa 6)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"ammonium N–H (pKa 9.17)"}],"t":"Histidine, fully protonated","fig":{"sites":224},"ex":"Imidazolium (6.0) is the group that lets histidine buffer near pH 7 in proteins."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in water?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"carboxylic acid O–H (pKa 2.2)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"phenol O–H (pKa 10.07)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"ammonium N–H (pKa 9.11)"}],"t":"Tyrosine, fully protonated","fig":{"sites":225},"ex":"Order of loss: CO₂H, then NH₃⁺, then the phenol."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in water?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"carboxylic acid O–H (pKa 1.96)"},{"pre":"S–H","x":"thiol S–H (pKa 8.18)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"ammonium N–H (pKa 10.28)"}],"t":"Cysteine, fully protonated","fig":{"sites":226},"ex":"Order of loss: CO₂H, then the thiol, then NH₃⁺."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in water?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"carboxylic acid O–H (pKa 2.97)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"phenol O–H (pKa 13.6)"}],"t":"Salicylic acid","fig":{"sites":227},"ex":"An H-bond from the OH holds the carboxylate, so this is a strong benzoic acid. The same H-bond makes the phenol unusually weak."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in water?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"carboxylic acid O–H (pKa 3.5)"},{"pre":"C–H","x":"acetyl C–H, as in ethyl acetate (pKa ≈25)"}],"t":"Aspirin","fig":{"sites":228},"ex":"Its only acidic group in water is the carboxylic acid."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in water?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"phenol O–H (pKa 9.5)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"amide N–H, as in acetamide (pKa ≈15)"}],"t":"Paracetamol","fig":{"sites":229},"ex":"Paracetamol is a phenol. The amide N–H is far weaker."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in DMSO?","o":[{"pre":"N–H","x":"sulfonamide N–H, as in benzenesulfonamide (pKa ≈16)"},{"pre":"N–H","x":"aniline N–H, as in aniline (pKa ≈31)"}],"t":"Sulfanilamide","fig":{"sites":230},"ex":"The sulfonyl group spreads the N⁻ charge onto two oxygens. In water the sulfonamide pKa is about 10."},{"s":"pka","k":"Most acidic proton","a":"Which labelled proton is the most acidic in water?","o":[{"pre":"O–H","x":"enol O–H at C3 (pKa 4.17)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"enol O–H at C2 (pKa 11.6)"},{"pre":"O–H","x":"alcohol O–H, as in ethanol (pKa ≈16)"}],"t":"Ascorbic acid (vitamin C)","fig":{"sites":231},"ex":"The C3 anion is a vinylogous carboxylate: its charge reaches the lactone C=O through the C=C."},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":232},{"m":233},{"m":234},{"m":235}],"t":"Aldol reaction","fig":{"r":[236,237],"cond":["LDA, THF, −78 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"An enolate adds to an aldehyde or ketone, giving a β-hydroxy carbonyl compound. Heating eliminates water to give the enone (aldol condensation).","mc":["LDA removes an α-proton, giving the lithium enolate.","The enolate carbon adds to the aldehyde carbonyl through a six-membered, chair-like transition state (Zimmerman–Traxler).","Work-up protonates the lithium alkoxide, giving the β-hydroxy carbonyl compound."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Aldol reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldol_reaction#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Aldol reaction"},{"x":"Morita–Baylis–Hillman reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Chaykovsky reaction"},{"x":"Darzens reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[236,237],"cond":["LDA, THF, −78 °C"],"p":238},"ex":"Aldol reaction: An enolate adds to an aldehyde or ketone, giving a β-hydroxy carbonyl compound. Heating eliminates water to give the enone (aldol condensation).","mc":["LDA removes an α-proton, giving the lithium enolate.","The enolate carbon adds to the aldehyde carbonyl through a six-membered, chair-like transition state (Zimmerman–Traxler).","Work-up protonates the lithium alkoxide, giving the β-hydroxy carbonyl compound."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Aldol reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldol_reaction#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":239},{"m":240},{"m":241},{"m":242}],"t":"Claisen condensation","fig":{"r":[243,244],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"Two esters combine under alkoxide base to give a β-keto ester. Driven to completion by deprotonating the acidic β-keto ester product.","mc":["Ethoxide removes an α-proton from one ester, giving an enolate.","The enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second ester, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","The intermediate collapses and expels ethoxide, giving the β-keto ester.","Ethoxide deprotonates the CH₂ between the two carbonyls (pKa ≈ 11), which drives the equilibrium; acid work-up reprotonates it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Claisen condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claisen_condensation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Claisen condensation"},{"x":"Reformatsky reaction"},{"x":"Darzens reaction"},{"x":"Barbier reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[243,244],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH","H₃O⁺"],"p":245},"ex":"Claisen condensation: Two esters combine under alkoxide base to give a β-keto ester. Driven to completion by deprotonating the acidic β-keto ester product.","mc":["Ethoxide removes an α-proton from one ester, giving an enolate.","The enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second ester, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","The intermediate collapses and expels ethoxide, giving the β-keto ester.","Ethoxide deprotonates the CH₂ between the two carbonyls (pKa ≈ 11), which drives the equilibrium; acid work-up reprotonates it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Claisen condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claisen_condensation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":246},{"m":247},{"m":248},{"m":249}],"t":"Dieckmann condensation","fig":{"r":[250],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"An intramolecular Claisen condensation: a diester closes to a cyclic β-keto ester. Works best for five- and six-membered rings.","mc":["Ethoxide forms an enolate at the α-carbon of one ester group.","The enolate attacks the other ester carbonyl of the same molecule, closing a five-membered ring.","Loss of ethoxide gives the cyclic β-keto ester, which is deprotonated (the driving force) and reprotonated on work-up."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dieckmann condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dieckmann_condensation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Dieckmann condensation"},{"x":"Benzoin condensation"},{"x":"Knoevenagel condensation"},{"x":"Mukaiyama aldol reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[250],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH","H₃O⁺"],"p":251},"ex":"Dieckmann condensation: An intramolecular Claisen condensation: a diester closes to a cyclic β-keto ester. Works best for five- and six-membered rings.","mc":["Ethoxide forms an enolate at the α-carbon of one ester group.","The enolate attacks the other ester carbonyl of the same molecule, closing a five-membered ring.","Loss of ethoxide gives the cyclic β-keto ester, which is deprotonated (the driving force) and reprotonated on work-up."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dieckmann condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dieckmann_condensation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":252},{"m":253},{"m":254},{"m":255}],"t":"Michael addition","fig":{"r":[256,257],"cond":["NaOEt (cat.), EtOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"A stabilised carbanion adds to the β-carbon of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl (1,4-addition).","mc":["Ethoxide deprotonates the malonate, giving a stabilised enolate.","The enolate adds to the β-carbon of the enone (1,4-addition), forming a new enolate.","Proton transfer from ethanol gives the product and regenerates ethoxide, so the base is catalytic."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Michael addition: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_addition_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Michael addition"},{"x":"Corey–Chaykovsky reaction"},{"x":"Benzoin condensation"},{"x":"Grignard reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[256,257],"cond":["NaOEt (cat.), EtOH"],"p":258},"ex":"Michael addition: A stabilised carbanion adds to the β-carbon of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl (1,4-addition).","mc":["Ethoxide deprotonates the malonate, giving a stabilised enolate.","The enolate adds to the β-carbon of the enone (1,4-addition), forming a new enolate.","Proton transfer from ethanol gives the product and regenerates ethoxide, so the base is catalytic."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Michael addition: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_addition_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":259},{"m":260},{"m":261},{"m":262}],"t":"Robinson annulation","fig":{"r":[263,264],"cond":["NaOH, EtOH, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A Michael addition followed by an intramolecular aldol condensation builds a new cyclohexenone ring. Used to make the Wieland–Miescher ketone and in many steroid syntheses.","mc":["Hydroxide forms the enolate of cyclohexanone, which adds 1,4 to methyl vinyl ketone (Michael addition).","The resulting 1,5-diketone forms an enolate at its methyl ketone.","This enolate attacks the ring carbonyl (intramolecular aldol), closing the new six-membered ring.","Dehydration of the β-hydroxy ketone (E1cB) gives the conjugated cyclohexenone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Robinson annulation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robinson_annulation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Robinson annulation"},{"x":"Darzens reaction"},{"x":"Grignard reaction"},{"x":"Reformatsky reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[263,264],"cond":["NaOH, EtOH, heat"],"p":265},"ex":"Robinson annulation: A Michael addition followed by an intramolecular aldol condensation builds a new cyclohexenone ring. Used to make the Wieland–Miescher ketone and in many steroid syntheses.","mc":["Hydroxide forms the enolate of cyclohexanone, which adds 1,4 to methyl vinyl ketone (Michael addition).","The resulting 1,5-diketone forms an enolate at its methyl ketone.","This enolate attacks the ring carbonyl (intramolecular aldol), closing the new six-membered ring.","Dehydration of the β-hydroxy ketone (E1cB) gives the conjugated cyclohexenone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Robinson annulation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robinson_annulation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":266},{"m":267},{"m":268},{"m":269}],"t":"Mannich reaction","fig":{"r":[270,271,272],"cond":["HCl (cat.), EtOH, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An enolisable carbonyl, an aldehyde and an amine combine to a β-amino carbonyl compound (Mannich base). The electrophile is an iminium ion formed from the aldehyde and the amine.","mc":["Dimethylamine condenses with formaldehyde to the iminium ion CH₂=NMe₂⁺.","Under the acidic conditions acetone tautomerises to its enol.","The enol attacks the iminium carbon; loss of a proton gives the β-amino ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Mannich reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mannich_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Mannich reaction"},{"x":"Knoevenagel condensation"},{"x":"Corey–Chaykovsky reaction"},{"x":"Aldol reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[270,271,272],"cond":["HCl (cat.), EtOH, heat"],"p":273},"ex":"Mannich reaction: An enolisable carbonyl, an aldehyde and an amine combine to a β-amino carbonyl compound (Mannich base). The electrophile is an iminium ion formed from the aldehyde and the amine.","mc":["Dimethylamine condenses with formaldehyde to the iminium ion CH₂=NMe₂⁺.","Under the acidic conditions acetone tautomerises to its enol.","The enol attacks the iminium carbon; loss of a proton gives the β-amino ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Mannich reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mannich_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":274},{"m":275},{"m":276},{"m":277}],"t":"Grignard reaction","fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["CH₃MgBr, Et₂O","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"An organomagnesium halide adds to a carbonyl group to give an alcohol. Needs dry conditions: water destroys Grignard reagents.","mc":["Magnesium coordinates to the carbonyl oxygen; the polar C–Mg bond makes the methyl carbon nucleophilic.","The methyl group adds to the carbonyl carbon, giving a magnesium alkoxide.","Aqueous acid protonates the alkoxide to the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Grignard reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grignard_reaction#Mechanism_and_stereochemistry"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Grignard reaction"},{"x":"Robinson annulation"},{"x":"Corey–Chaykovsky reaction"},{"x":"Aldol reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["CH₃MgBr, Et₂O","H₃O⁺"],"p":279},"ex":"Grignard reaction: An organomagnesium halide adds to a carbonyl group to give an alcohol. Needs dry conditions: water destroys Grignard reagents.","mc":["Magnesium coordinates to the carbonyl oxygen; the polar C–Mg bond makes the methyl carbon nucleophilic.","The methyl group adds to the carbonyl carbon, giving a magnesium alkoxide.","Aqueous acid protonates the alkoxide to the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Grignard reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grignard_reaction#Mechanism_and_stereochemistry"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":280},{"m":281},{"m":282},{"m":283}],"t":"Barbier reaction","fig":{"r":[284,285],"cond":["Zn, THF/sat. aq. NH₄Cl, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"A metal, an alkyl halide and a carbonyl compound react in one pot: the organometallic forms in the presence of the carbonyl and adds to it at once. Unlike a Grignard reagent, the organometallic is never made separately. Zinc, indium and tin tolerate water; SmI₂ also runs Barbier reactions.","mc":["Zinc inserts into the C–Br bond of allyl bromide, giving an allylzinc bromide alongside the aldehyde.","The allylzinc adds to the carbonyl through a cyclic six-membered transition state.","The aqueous ammonium chloride protonates the zinc alkoxide, giving the homoallylic alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Barbier reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbier_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Barbier reaction"},{"x":"Mukaiyama aldol reaction"},{"x":"Claisen condensation"},{"x":"Benzoin condensation"}],"fig":{"r":[284,285],"cond":["Zn, THF/sat. aq. NH₄Cl, rt"],"p":286},"ex":"Barbier reaction: A metal, an alkyl halide and a carbonyl compound react in one pot: the organometallic forms in the presence of the carbonyl and adds to it at once. Unlike a Grignard reagent, the organometallic is never made separately. Zinc, indium and tin tolerate water; SmI₂ also runs Barbier reactions.","mc":["Zinc inserts into the C–Br bond of allyl bromide, giving an allylzinc bromide alongside the aldehyde.","The allylzinc adds to the carbonyl through a cyclic six-membered transition state.","The aqueous ammonium chloride protonates the zinc alkoxide, giving the homoallylic alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Barbier reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbier_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":287},{"m":288},{"m":289},{"m":290}],"t":"Reformatsky reaction","fig":{"r":[291,278],"cond":["Zn, THF","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"A zinc enolate made from an α-bromo ester adds to an aldehyde or ketone, giving a β-hydroxy ester. Zinc enolates are too mild to attack the ester group.","mc":["Zinc inserts into the C–Br bond (oxidative addition), giving a zinc enolate.","The zinc enolate adds to the aldehyde through a six-membered chair transition state.","Acid work-up protonates the zinc alkoxide to the β-hydroxy ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Reformatsky reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reformatsky_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Reformatsky reaction"},{"x":"Stork enamine alkylation"},{"x":"Mannich reaction"},{"x":"Dieckmann condensation"}],"fig":{"r":[291,278],"cond":["Zn, THF","H₃O⁺"],"p":292},"ex":"Reformatsky reaction: A zinc enolate made from an α-bromo ester adds to an aldehyde or ketone, giving a β-hydroxy ester. Zinc enolates are too mild to attack the ester group.","mc":["Zinc inserts into the C–Br bond (oxidative addition), giving a zinc enolate.","The zinc enolate adds to the aldehyde through a six-membered chair transition state.","Acid work-up protonates the zinc alkoxide to the β-hydroxy ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Reformatsky reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reformatsky_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":293},{"m":294},{"m":295},{"m":296}],"t":"Knoevenagel condensation","fig":{"r":[278,256],"cond":["piperidine (cat.), AcOH, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aldehyde condenses with an active methylene compound such as a malonate to an electron-poor alkene.","mc":["Piperidine and the aldehyde form an iminium ion, which is more electrophilic than the aldehyde.","The malonate, deprotonated by the amine, adds to the iminium ion.","Elimination of piperidine (E1cB) gives the conjugated alkylidene malonate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Knoevenagel condensation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knoevenagel_condensation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Knoevenagel condensation"},{"x":"Aldol reaction"},{"x":"Mukaiyama aldol reaction"},{"x":"Dieckmann condensation"}],"fig":{"r":[278,256],"cond":["piperidine (cat.), AcOH, heat"],"p":297},"ex":"Knoevenagel condensation: An aldehyde condenses with an active methylene compound such as a malonate to an electron-poor alkene.","mc":["Piperidine and the aldehyde form an iminium ion, which is more electrophilic than the aldehyde.","The malonate, deprotonated by the amine, adds to the iminium ion.","Elimination of piperidine (E1cB) gives the conjugated alkylidene malonate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Knoevenagel condensation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knoevenagel_condensation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":298},{"m":299},{"m":300},{"m":301}],"t":"Henry reaction","fig":{"r":[278,302],"cond":["Et₃N (or another base)"],"veil":true},"ex":"A nitroalkane adds to an aldehyde under base, giving a β-nitro alcohol.","mc":["Base removes the acidic α-proton of nitromethane (pKa ≈ 10), giving a nitronate.","The nitronate carbon adds to the aldehyde carbonyl.","The alkoxide is protonated, giving the β-nitro alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Henry reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Henry reaction"},{"x":"Benzoin condensation"},{"x":"Grignard reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Chaykovsky reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[278,302],"cond":["Et₃N (or another base)"],"p":303},"ex":"Henry reaction: A nitroalkane adds to an aldehyde under base, giving a β-nitro alcohol.","mc":["Base removes the acidic α-proton of nitromethane (pKa ≈ 10), giving a nitronate.","The nitronate carbon adds to the aldehyde carbonyl.","The alkoxide is protonated, giving the β-nitro alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Henry reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":304},{"m":305},{"m":306},{"m":307}],"t":"Morita–Baylis–Hillman reaction","fig":{"r":[278,308],"cond":["DABCO (cat.)"],"veil":true},"ex":"A nucleophilic amine catalyst couples the α-position of an activated alkene to an aldehyde. Slow, but atom-economical and needs no strong base.","mc":["DABCO adds 1,4 to the acrylate, giving a zwitterionic enolate.","The enolate attacks the aldehyde (an aldol-type addition).","Proton transfer and elimination of DABCO (E1cB) restore the alkene and release the catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Morita–Baylis–Hillman reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baylis%E2%80%93Hillman_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Morita–Baylis–Hillman reaction"},{"x":"Mannich reaction"},{"x":"Stork enamine alkylation"},{"x":"Henry reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[278,308],"cond":["DABCO (cat.)"],"p":309},"ex":"Morita–Baylis–Hillman reaction: A nucleophilic amine catalyst couples the α-position of an activated alkene to an aldehyde. Slow, but atom-economical and needs no strong base.","mc":["DABCO adds 1,4 to the acrylate, giving a zwitterionic enolate.","The enolate attacks the aldehyde (an aldol-type addition).","Proton transfer and elimination of DABCO (E1cB) restore the alkene and release the catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Morita–Baylis–Hillman reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baylis%E2%80%93Hillman_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":310},{"m":311},{"m":312},{"m":313}],"t":"Stork enamine alkylation","fig":{"r":[314,284],"cond":["pyrrolidine, TsOH (enamine)","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"A ketone is turned into an enamine, alkylated at its α-carbon, then hydrolysed back to the ketone. Avoids the strong bases and over-alkylation of direct enolate alkylation.","mc":["Pyrrolidine and the ketone condense, with loss of water, to the enamine.","The nucleophilic β-carbon of the enamine attacks allyl bromide (SN2), giving an iminium salt.","Aqueous acid hydrolyses the iminium ion back to the ketone and pyrrolidine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Stork enamine alkylation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stork_enamine_alkylation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Stork enamine alkylation"},{"x":"Robinson annulation"},{"x":"Darzens reaction"},{"x":"Morita–Baylis–Hillman reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[314,284],"cond":["pyrrolidine, TsOH (enamine)","H₃O⁺"],"p":315},"ex":"Stork enamine alkylation: A ketone is turned into an enamine, alkylated at its α-carbon, then hydrolysed back to the ketone. Avoids the strong bases and over-alkylation of direct enolate alkylation.","mc":["Pyrrolidine and the ketone condense, with loss of water, to the enamine.","The nucleophilic β-carbon of the enamine attacks allyl bromide (SN2), giving an iminium salt.","Aqueous acid hydrolyses the iminium ion back to the ketone and pyrrolidine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Stork enamine alkylation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stork_enamine_alkylation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":316},{"m":317},{"m":318},{"m":319}],"t":"Benzoin condensation","fig":{"r":[285,320],"cond":["KCN (cat.), EtOH, H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Cyanide or an NHC couples two aryl aldehydes into an α-hydroxy ketone (umpolung). Cyanide reverses the polarity of the aldehyde carbon so it can act as a nucleophile.","mc":["Cyanide adds to one benzaldehyde, giving a cyanohydrin alkoxide.","Proton transfer gives a carbanion stabilised by the nitrile and the ring: the former carbonyl carbon is now nucleophilic (umpolung).","This carbanion adds to a second benzaldehyde.","Proton transfer and loss of cyanide reform the C=O, giving benzoin and releasing the catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Benzoin condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benzoin_condensation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Benzoin condensation"},{"x":"Claisen condensation"},{"x":"Barbier reaction"},{"x":"Aldol reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[285,320],"cond":["KCN (cat.), EtOH, H₂O"],"p":321},"ex":"Benzoin condensation: Cyanide or an NHC couples two aryl aldehydes into an α-hydroxy ketone (umpolung). Cyanide reverses the polarity of the aldehyde carbon so it can act as a nucleophile.","mc":["Cyanide adds to one benzaldehyde, giving a cyanohydrin alkoxide.","Proton transfer gives a carbanion stabilised by the nitrile and the ring: the former carbonyl carbon is now nucleophilic (umpolung).","This carbanion adds to a second benzaldehyde.","Proton transfer and loss of cyanide reform the C=O, giving benzoin and releasing the catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Benzoin condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benzoin_condensation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":322},{"m":323},{"m":324},{"m":325}],"t":"Corey–Chaykovsky reaction","fig":{"r":[263],"cond":["Me₃S(O)⁺ I⁻, NaH, DMSO"],"veil":true},"ex":"A sulfur ylide adds to a ketone and closes to an epoxide (transfer of CH₂). With enones the sulfoxonium ylide gives cyclopropanes instead.","mc":["NaH deprotonates trimethylsulfoxonium iodide to the ylide Me₂S(O)=CH₂.","The ylide carbon adds to the ketone carbonyl, giving a betaine: an alkoxide next to a sulfoxonium group.","The alkoxide displaces DMSO intramolecularly (SN2), closing the epoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Chaykovsky reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnson%E2%80%93Corey%E2%80%93Chaykovsky_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Corey–Chaykovsky reaction"},{"x":"Stork enamine alkylation"},{"x":"Aldol reaction"},{"x":"Henry reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[263],"cond":["Me₃S(O)⁺ I⁻, NaH, DMSO"],"p":326},"ex":"Corey–Chaykovsky reaction: A sulfur ylide adds to a ketone and closes to an epoxide (transfer of CH₂). With enones the sulfoxonium ylide gives cyclopropanes instead.","mc":["NaH deprotonates trimethylsulfoxonium iodide to the ylide Me₂S(O)=CH₂.","The ylide carbon adds to the ketone carbonyl, giving a betaine: an alkoxide next to a sulfoxonium group.","The alkoxide displaces DMSO intramolecularly (SN2), closing the epoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Chaykovsky reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnson%E2%80%93Corey%E2%80%93Chaykovsky_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":327},{"m":328},{"m":329},{"m":330}],"t":"Mukaiyama aldol reaction","fig":{"r":[331,278],"cond":["TiCl₄, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A silyl enol ether adds to an aldehyde activated by a Lewis acid.","mc":["TiCl₄ coordinates to the aldehyde oxygen, making the carbonyl strongly electrophilic.","The silyl enol ether attacks the activated aldehyde through an open transition state.","Loss of the silyl group and aqueous work-up give the β-hydroxy ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Mukaiyama aldol reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mukaiyama_aldol_addition#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Mukaiyama aldol reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Chaykovsky reaction"},{"x":"Claisen condensation"},{"x":"Benzoin condensation"}],"fig":{"r":[331,278],"cond":["TiCl₄, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"],"p":332},"ex":"Mukaiyama aldol reaction: A silyl enol ether adds to an aldehyde activated by a Lewis acid.","mc":["TiCl₄ coordinates to the aldehyde oxygen, making the carbonyl strongly electrophilic.","The silyl enol ether attacks the activated aldehyde through an open transition state.","Loss of the silyl group and aqueous work-up give the β-hydroxy ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Mukaiyama aldol reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mukaiyama_aldol_addition#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":333},{"m":334},{"m":335},{"m":336}],"t":"Darzens reaction","fig":{"r":[337,338],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"An α-halo ester and a ketone give an α,β-epoxy ester (glycidic ester).","mc":["Ethoxide deprotonates the α-chloro ester to its enolate.","The enolate adds to the ketone, giving a chlorohydrin alkoxide.","The alkoxide displaces chloride intramolecularly (SN2), closing the epoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Darzens reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darzens_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Darzens reaction"},{"x":"Morita–Baylis–Hillman reaction"},{"x":"Mukaiyama aldol reaction"},{"x":"Dieckmann condensation"}],"fig":{"r":[337,338],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH"],"p":339},"ex":"Darzens reaction: An α-halo ester and a ketone give an α,β-epoxy ester (glycidic ester).","mc":["Ethoxide deprotonates the α-chloro ester to its enolate.","The enolate adds to the ketone, giving a chlorohydrin alkoxide.","The alkoxide displaces chloride intramolecularly (SN2), closing the epoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Darzens reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darzens_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":340},{"m":341},{"m":342},{"m":343}],"t":"Prins reaction","fig":{"r":[344,271,271],"cond":["HCHO (excess), H₂SO₄, H₂O, 50 °C"],"veil":true,"key":["HCHO (excess)"]},"ex":"Acid-catalysed addition of an aldehyde to an alkene through a carbocation; what the cation does next depends on the conditions. With excess formaldehyde below 70 °C the cation is trapped as a 1,3-dioxane; with one equivalent and more heat 1,3-diols or allylic alcohols form instead (the allylic alcohol is the same product as an ene reaction would give).","mc":["Acid protonates formaldehyde; the alkene attacks it, giving the more stable benzylic cation.","A second formaldehyde adds to the cation through its oxygen, giving an oxocarbenium ion.","The new hydroxyl group closes the ring onto it; loss of a proton gives the 1,3-dioxane."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Prins reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prins_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Prins reaction"},{"x":"Grignard reaction"},{"x":"Dieckmann condensation"},{"x":"Benzoin condensation"}],"fig":{"r":[344,271,271],"cond":["HCHO (excess), H₂SO₄, H₂O, 50 °C"],"p":345,"key":["HCHO (excess)"]},"ex":"Prins reaction: Acid-catalysed addition of an aldehyde to an alkene through a carbocation; what the cation does next depends on the conditions. With excess formaldehyde below 70 °C the cation is trapped as a 1,3-dioxane; with one equivalent and more heat 1,3-diols or allylic alcohols form instead (the allylic alcohol is the same product as an ene reaction would give).","mc":["Acid protonates formaldehyde; the alkene attacks it, giving the more stable benzylic cation.","A second formaldehyde adds to the cation through its oxygen, giving an oxocarbenium ion.","The new hydroxyl group closes the ring onto it; loss of a proton gives the 1,3-dioxane."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Prins reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prins_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":346},{"m":347},{"m":348},{"m":349}],"t":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis","fig":{"r":[350,351],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH; then BuBr","NaOH (aq); then H₃O⁺, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated at its acidic CH₂; hydrolysis and decarboxylation then give a methyl ketone, RCH₂COCH₃. The β-keto acid loses CO₂ easily on heating. With two equivalents of a strong base the dianion forms and alkylates at the terminal methyl group instead.","mc":["Ethoxide removes a proton from the CH₂ between the two carbonyls (pKa about 11), giving a stabilised enolate.","The enolate is alkylated on carbon by butyl bromide (SN2).","Hydrolysis gives the β-keto acid, which loses CO₂ on heating through a cyclic six-membered transition state.","The enol formed tautomerises to the methyl ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Acetoacetic ester synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acetoacetic_ester_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis"},{"x":"Malonic ester synthesis"},{"x":"Aldol condensation"},{"x":"Conia-ene reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[350,351],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH; then BuBr","NaOH (aq); then H₃O⁺, heat"],"p":352},"ex":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis: Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated at its acidic CH₂; hydrolysis and decarboxylation then give a methyl ketone, RCH₂COCH₃. The β-keto acid loses CO₂ easily on heating. With two equivalents of a strong base the dianion forms and alkylates at the terminal methyl group instead.","mc":["Ethoxide removes a proton from the CH₂ between the two carbonyls (pKa about 11), giving a stabilised enolate.","The enolate is alkylated on carbon by butyl bromide (SN2).","Hydrolysis gives the β-keto acid, which loses CO₂ on heating through a cyclic six-membered transition state.","The enol formed tautomerises to the methyl ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Acetoacetic ester synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acetoacetic_ester_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":353},{"m":354},{"m":355},{"m":356}],"t":"Malonic ester synthesis","fig":{"r":[357,358],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH; then BnBr","NaOH (aq); then H₃O⁺, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"Diethyl malonate is alkylated at its CH₂; hydrolysis and decarboxylation then give a carboxylic acid RCH₂CO₂H. Ethoxide is basic enough (malonate pKa about 13). Excess malonate avoids dialkylation; a dihalide alkylates twice and closes a ring, even a cyclobutane.","mc":["Ethoxide removes a proton from the CH₂ between the two esters, giving a stabilised enolate.","The enolate is alkylated on carbon by benzyl bromide (SN2).","Hydrolysis gives the malonic acid, which loses CO₂ on heating through a cyclic transition state.","The enol of the acid tautomerises, giving the substituted acetic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Malonic ester synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malonic_ester_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Malonic ester synthesis"},{"x":"Aldol condensation"},{"x":"Stetter reaction"},{"x":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[357,358],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH; then BnBr","NaOH (aq); then H₃O⁺, heat"],"p":359},"ex":"Malonic ester synthesis: Diethyl malonate is alkylated at its CH₂; hydrolysis and decarboxylation then give a carboxylic acid RCH₂CO₂H. Ethoxide is basic enough (malonate pKa about 13). Excess malonate avoids dialkylation; a dihalide alkylates twice and closes a ring, even a cyclobutane.","mc":["Ethoxide removes a proton from the CH₂ between the two esters, giving a stabilised enolate.","The enolate is alkylated on carbon by benzyl bromide (SN2).","Hydrolysis gives the malonic acid, which loses CO₂ on heating through a cyclic transition state.","The enol of the acid tautomerises, giving the substituted acetic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Malonic ester synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malonic_ester_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":360},{"m":361},{"m":362},{"m":363}],"t":"Aldol condensation","fig":{"r":[364,285],"cond":["NaOH, EtOH/H₂O, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aldol addition followed by loss of water: the enolate adds to a carbonyl compound and the aldol dehydrates to the conjugated enone. Conjugation drives the dehydration. Benzaldehyde has no α-hydrogen, so only one crossed product forms (here chalcone).","mc":["Hydroxide removes an α-hydrogen of acetophenone, giving the enolate.","The enolate adds to benzaldehyde; protonation gives the β-hydroxy ketone (the aldol).","A second deprotonation gives the enolate of the aldol, which expels hydroxide (E1cB), giving the conjugated E-enone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Aldol condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldol_condensation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Aldol condensation"},{"x":"Stetter reaction"},{"x":"O'Donnell amino acid synthesis"},{"x":"Corey–Seebach reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[364,285],"cond":["NaOH, EtOH/H₂O, rt"],"p":365},"ex":"Aldol condensation: An aldol addition followed by loss of water: the enolate adds to a carbonyl compound and the aldol dehydrates to the conjugated enone. Conjugation drives the dehydration. Benzaldehyde has no α-hydrogen, so only one crossed product forms (here chalcone).","mc":["Hydroxide removes an α-hydrogen of acetophenone, giving the enolate.","The enolate adds to benzaldehyde; protonation gives the β-hydroxy ketone (the aldol).","A second deprotonation gives the enolate of the aldol, which expels hydroxide (E1cB), giving the conjugated E-enone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Aldol condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldol_condensation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":366},{"m":367},{"m":368},{"m":369}],"t":"Knoevenagel–Doebner condensation","fig":{"r":[320,370],"cond":["malonic acid, pyridine, piperidine (cat.), reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"A Knoevenagel condensation with malonic acid in pyridine: the condensation is followed by decarboxylation, giving the α,β-unsaturated acid. In refluxing pyridine decarboxylation and elimination happen together; the classic route to cinnamic acids.","mc":["Piperidine and the aldehyde form an iminium ion, which the enol of malonic acid attacks.","This gives the addition product of the malonic acid and the aldehyde.","In hot pyridine, loss of CO₂ and of the leaving group happen together (decarboxylative elimination), giving (E)-cinnamic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Knoevenagel condensation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knoevenagel_condensation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Knoevenagel–Doebner condensation"},{"x":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis"},{"x":"Corey–Seebach reaction"},{"x":"Aldol condensation"}],"fig":{"r":[320,370],"cond":["malonic acid, pyridine, piperidine (cat.), reflux"],"p":371},"ex":"Knoevenagel–Doebner condensation: A Knoevenagel condensation with malonic acid in pyridine: the condensation is followed by decarboxylation, giving the α,β-unsaturated acid. In refluxing pyridine decarboxylation and elimination happen together; the classic route to cinnamic acids.","mc":["Piperidine and the aldehyde form an iminium ion, which the enol of malonic acid attacks.","This gives the addition product of the malonic acid and the aldehyde.","In hot pyridine, loss of CO₂ and of the leaving group happen together (decarboxylative elimination), giving (E)-cinnamic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Knoevenagel condensation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knoevenagel_condensation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":372},{"m":373},{"m":374},{"m":375}],"t":"Blaise reaction","fig":{"r":[376,291],"cond":["Zn (activated), THF, reflux","50% aq. K₂CO₃"],"veil":true,"key":["50% aq. K₂CO₃"]},"ex":"The zinc enolate of an α-bromo ester adds to a nitrile; the work-up gives a β-enamino ester or, with acid, a β-keto ester. Activated zinc, THF and slow addition of excess bromo ester avoid the self-condensation that made the original method low-yielding. A basic work-up keeps the enamine, held Z by an N–H···O=C hydrogen bond.","mc":["Zinc inserts into the C–Br bond of ethyl bromoacetate, giving a zinc enolate (a Reformatsky reagent).","The zinc enolate adds to the nitrile carbon, giving the zinc salt of an imine (the Blaise intermediate).","Basic aqueous work-up protonates it to the conjugated β-enamino ester; aqueous acid instead hydrolyses it to the β-keto ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Blaise reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blaise_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":377},{"m":372},{"m":378},{"m":379}],"t":"Blaise reaction","fig":{"r":[376,291],"cond":["Zn (activated), THF, reflux","50% aq. K₂CO₃, then 1 M HCl"],"veil":true,"key":["1 M HCl"]},"ex":"The zinc enolate of an α-bromo ester adds to a nitrile; the work-up gives a β-enamino ester or, with acid, a β-keto ester. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the enamine, through the iminium ion, to the ketone: the β-keto ester.","mc":["Zinc inserts into the C–Br bond of ethyl bromoacetate, giving a zinc enolate (a Reformatsky reagent).","The zinc enolate adds to the nitrile carbon, giving the zinc salt of an imine (the Blaise intermediate).","Basic aqueous work-up protonates it to the conjugated β-enamino ester; aqueous acid instead hydrolyses it to the β-keto ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Blaise reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blaise_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Blaise reaction"},{"x":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement"},{"x":"Stetter reaction"},{"x":"Malonic ester synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[376,291],"cond":["Zn (activated), THF, reflux","50% aq. K₂CO₃"],"p":380,"key":["50% aq. K₂CO₃"]},"ex":"Blaise reaction: The zinc enolate of an α-bromo ester adds to a nitrile; the work-up gives a β-enamino ester or, with acid, a β-keto ester. Activated zinc, THF and slow addition of excess bromo ester avoid the self-condensation that made the original method low-yielding. A basic work-up keeps the enamine, held Z by an N–H···O=C hydrogen bond.","mc":["Zinc inserts into the C–Br bond of ethyl bromoacetate, giving a zinc enolate (a Reformatsky reagent).","The zinc enolate adds to the nitrile carbon, giving the zinc salt of an imine (the Blaise intermediate).","Basic aqueous work-up protonates it to the conjugated β-enamino ester; aqueous acid instead hydrolyses it to the β-keto ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Blaise reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blaise_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":381},{"m":382},{"m":383},{"m":384}],"t":"Ivanov reaction","fig":{"r":[385,285],"cond":["i-PrMgCl (2 equiv), Et₂O","PhCHO","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"The dianion (enediolate) of an arylacetic acid adds to an aldehyde, giving a β-hydroxy acid, like an aldol addition. A chair-like Zimmerman–Traxler transition state favours the anti diastereomer.","mc":["Two equivalents of the Grignard reagent remove the O–H and an α-C–H of phenylacetic acid, giving the magnesium enediolate (the Ivanov reagent).","The enediolate adds to benzaldehyde through a chair-like six-membered transition state (Zimmerman–Traxler).","Acidic work-up gives the β-hydroxy acid, mainly the anti diastereomer."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ivanov reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivanov_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ivanov reaction"},{"x":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement"},{"x":"Conia-ene reaction"},{"x":"Acyloin condensation"}],"fig":{"r":[385,285],"cond":["i-PrMgCl (2 equiv), Et₂O","PhCHO","H₃O⁺"],"p":386},"ex":"Ivanov reaction: The dianion (enediolate) of an arylacetic acid adds to an aldehyde, giving a β-hydroxy acid, like an aldol addition. A chair-like Zimmerman–Traxler transition state favours the anti diastereomer.","mc":["Two equivalents of the Grignard reagent remove the O–H and an α-C–H of phenylacetic acid, giving the magnesium enediolate (the Ivanov reagent).","The enediolate adds to benzaldehyde through a chair-like six-membered transition state (Zimmerman–Traxler).","Acidic work-up gives the β-hydroxy acid, mainly the anti diastereomer."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ivanov reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivanov_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":387},{"m":388},{"m":389},{"m":390}],"t":"O'Donnell amino acid synthesis","fig":{"r":[391,358],"cond":["BnBr, 50% aq. NaOH, Bu₄N⁺ Br⁻ (cat.), toluene"],"veil":true},"ex":"The benzophenone imine of a glycine ester is alkylated under phase-transfer conditions; hydrolysis then gives an α-amino acid. The monoalkylated imine is much less acidic than the starting imine, so it is neither alkylated again nor racemised. Chiral (cinchona-derived) ammonium salts make the alkylation enantioselective.","mc":["At the interface between the phases, hydroxide removes the α-proton, made acidic by the ester and the imine.","The quaternary ammonium cation carries the enolate into the organic phase as an ion pair.","The enolate is alkylated by benzyl bromide (SN2), giving the monoalkylated Schiff base."],"lk":[{"h":"Organic Chemistry Portal","l":[["O'Donnell amino acid synthesis","https://www.organic-chemistry.org/namedreactions/o-donnell-amino-acid-synthesis.shtm"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"O'Donnell amino acid synthesis"},{"x":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis"},{"x":"Corey–Seebach reaction"},{"x":"Malonic ester synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[391,358],"cond":["BnBr, 50% aq. NaOH, Bu₄N⁺ Br⁻ (cat.), toluene"],"p":392},"ex":"O'Donnell amino acid synthesis: The benzophenone imine of a glycine ester is alkylated under phase-transfer conditions; hydrolysis then gives an α-amino acid. The monoalkylated imine is much less acidic than the starting imine, so it is neither alkylated again nor racemised. Chiral (cinchona-derived) ammonium salts make the alkylation enantioselective.","mc":["At the interface between the phases, hydroxide removes the α-proton, made acidic by the ester and the imine.","The quaternary ammonium cation carries the enolate into the organic phase as an ion pair.","The enolate is alkylated by benzyl bromide (SN2), giving the monoalkylated Schiff base."],"lk":[{"h":"Organic Chemistry Portal","l":[["O'Donnell amino acid synthesis","https://www.organic-chemistry.org/namedreactions/o-donnell-amino-acid-synthesis.shtm"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":393},{"m":394},{"m":395},{"m":396}],"t":"Corey–Seebach reaction","fig":{"r":[397,358],"cond":["n-BuLi, THF, −30 °C","BnBr","HgCl₂, CaCO₃, MeCN/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"A lithiated 1,3-dithiane is an acyl anion equivalent (umpolung): it is alkylated, and hydrolysis of the dithiane then reveals the ketone. Sulfur makes the C–H acidic (pKa about 30) through its polarisability, not through d-orbitals. Mercury(II) salts are needed to hydrolyse the stable thioacetal.","mc":["n-BuLi removes the C2–H of the dithiane, giving a 2-lithio-1,3-dithiane: a masked acyl anion.","The carbanion is alkylated by benzyl bromide (SN2).","Mercury(II) binds the sulfur atoms and water hydrolyses the thioacetal, revealing the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Seebach reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Seebach_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Corey–Seebach reaction"},{"x":"Stetter reaction"},{"x":"Ivanov reaction"},{"x":"Cannizzaro reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[397,358],"cond":["n-BuLi, THF, −30 °C","BnBr","HgCl₂, CaCO₃, MeCN/H₂O"],"p":398},"ex":"Corey–Seebach reaction: A lithiated 1,3-dithiane is an acyl anion equivalent (umpolung): it is alkylated, and hydrolysis of the dithiane then reveals the ketone. Sulfur makes the C–H acidic (pKa about 30) through its polarisability, not through d-orbitals. Mercury(II) salts are needed to hydrolyse the stable thioacetal.","mc":["n-BuLi removes the C2–H of the dithiane, giving a 2-lithio-1,3-dithiane: a masked acyl anion.","The carbanion is alkylated by benzyl bromide (SN2).","Mercury(II) binds the sulfur atoms and water hydrolyses the thioacetal, revealing the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Seebach reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Seebach_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":399},{"m":400},{"m":401},{"m":317}],"t":"Stetter reaction","fig":{"r":[320,257],"cond":["thiazolium salt (cat.), Et₃N, EtOH, 80 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A thiazolium-derived carbene (or cyanide) makes an aldehyde an acyl anion equivalent that adds 1,4 to a Michael acceptor, giving a 1,4-dicarbonyl compound. The competing 1,2-addition (benzoin condensation) is reversible, so the more stable 1,4-adduct wins. 1,4-Diketones are the substrates of the Paal–Knorr syntheses.","mc":["Base deprotonates the thiazolium salt to the carbene (ylide), which adds to the aldehyde.","Proton transfer gives the Breslow intermediate, an enaminol whose former carbonyl carbon is now nucleophilic (umpolung).","It adds 1,4 to methyl vinyl ketone; the alkoxide then releases the carbene, giving the 1,4-diketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Stetter reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stetter_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Stetter reaction"},{"x":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement"},{"x":"Malonic ester synthesis"},{"x":"Cannizzaro reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[320,257],"cond":["thiazolium salt (cat.), Et₃N, EtOH, 80 °C"],"p":402},"ex":"Stetter reaction: A thiazolium-derived carbene (or cyanide) makes an aldehyde an acyl anion equivalent that adds 1,4 to a Michael acceptor, giving a 1,4-dicarbonyl compound. The competing 1,2-addition (benzoin condensation) is reversible, so the more stable 1,4-adduct wins. 1,4-Diketones are the substrates of the Paal–Knorr syntheses.","mc":["Base deprotonates the thiazolium salt to the carbene (ylide), which adds to the aldehyde.","Proton transfer gives the Breslow intermediate, an enaminol whose former carbonyl carbon is now nucleophilic (umpolung).","It adds 1,4 to methyl vinyl ketone; the alkoxide then releases the carbene, giving the 1,4-diketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Stetter reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stetter_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":403},{"m":404},{"m":405},{"m":406}],"t":"Acyloin condensation","fig":{"r":[407,408],"cond":["Na, xylene, reflux","H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Sodium couples two esters at their carbonyl carbons, giving an α-hydroxy ketone (an acyloin). Especially useful intramolecularly, to close medium and large rings. Trapping the enediolate with Me₃SiCl improves the yield; with an alcohol present the esters are simply reduced to alcohols (Bouveault–Blanc).","mc":["Sodium transfers an electron to each ester carbonyl; the two radical anions couple.","The coupled dianion loses two ethoxide ions, giving a 1,2-diketone, which takes two more electrons to the enediolate.","Protonation on work-up gives the enediol, which tautomerises to the acyloin."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Acyloin condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acyloin_condensation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Acyloin condensation"},{"x":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement"},{"x":"Ivanov reaction"},{"x":"O'Donnell amino acid synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[407,408],"cond":["Na, xylene, reflux","H₂O"],"p":409},"ex":"Acyloin condensation: Sodium couples two esters at their carbonyl carbons, giving an α-hydroxy ketone (an acyloin). Especially useful intramolecularly, to close medium and large rings. Trapping the enediolate with Me₃SiCl improves the yield; with an alcohol present the esters are simply reduced to alcohols (Bouveault–Blanc).","mc":["Sodium transfers an electron to each ester carbonyl; the two radical anions couple.","The coupled dianion loses two ethoxide ions, giving a 1,2-diketone, which takes two more electrons to the enediolate.","Protonation on work-up gives the enediol, which tautomerises to the acyloin."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Acyloin condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acyloin_condensation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":410},{"m":411},{"m":412},{"m":413}],"t":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement","fig":{"r":[414],"cond":["KOH, pyridine, 50 °C","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"Base turns an o-acyloxy aryl ketone into a 1,3-diketone: the enolate attacks the neighbouring ester and the acyl group moves from oxygen to carbon. An intramolecular Claisen condensation. Acid then cyclises the 1,3-diketone to a flavone (here flavone itself).","mc":["Hydroxide removes a proton from the acetyl group, giving an enolate.","The enolate attacks the neighbouring ester carbonyl, forming a cyclic tetrahedral alkoxide.","The ring opens with the phenoxide as leaving group; acidic work-up gives the 1,3-diketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baker%E2%80%93Venkataraman_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement"},{"x":"Malonic ester synthesis"},{"x":"Conia-ene reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Seebach reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[414],"cond":["KOH, pyridine, 50 °C","H₃O⁺"],"p":415},"ex":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement: Base turns an o-acyloxy aryl ketone into a 1,3-diketone: the enolate attacks the neighbouring ester and the acyl group moves from oxygen to carbon. An intramolecular Claisen condensation. Acid then cyclises the 1,3-diketone to a flavone (here flavone itself).","mc":["Hydroxide removes a proton from the acetyl group, giving an enolate.","The enolate attacks the neighbouring ester carbonyl, forming a cyclic tetrahedral alkoxide.","The ring opens with the phenoxide as leaving group; acidic work-up gives the 1,3-diketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baker%E2%80%93Venkataraman_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":416},{"m":417},{"m":418},{"m":419}],"t":"Conia-ene reaction","fig":{"r":[420],"cond":["Ph₃PAuCl, AgOTf (1 mol%), CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"The enol of a carbonyl compound adds intramolecularly to an alkene or alkyne and closes a ring: an intramolecular ene reaction. Thermally it needs about 350 °C; β-keto esters with gold(I) or other Lewis acid catalysts react at room temperature. A terminal alkyne cyclises 5-exo, giving an exocyclic alkene.","mc":["Gold(I) coordinates the alkyne and makes it electrophilic.","The enol of the β-keto ester attacks the activated alkyne (5-exo-dig), giving a vinylgold intermediate.","Protodeauration releases the exocyclic methylene product and the gold catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Conia-ene reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conia-ene_reaction#History_and_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Conia-ene reaction"},{"x":"Aldol condensation"},{"x":"Cannizzaro reaction"},{"x":"Malonic ester synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[420],"cond":["Ph₃PAuCl, AgOTf (1 mol%), CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"p":421},"ex":"Conia-ene reaction: The enol of a carbonyl compound adds intramolecularly to an alkene or alkyne and closes a ring: an intramolecular ene reaction. Thermally it needs about 350 °C; β-keto esters with gold(I) or other Lewis acid catalysts react at room temperature. A terminal alkyne cyclises 5-exo, giving an exocyclic alkene.","mc":["Gold(I) coordinates the alkyne and makes it electrophilic.","The enol of the β-keto ester attacks the activated alkyne (5-exo-dig), giving a vinylgold intermediate.","Protodeauration releases the exocyclic methylene product and the gold catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Conia-ene reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conia-ene_reaction#History_and_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":422},{"m":423},{"m":424},{"m":425}],"t":"Kowalski ester homologation","fig":{"r":[426],"cond":["CH₂Br₂, LiTMP, THF, −78 °C","LiHMDS; then s-BuLi","HCl, EtOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"Turns an ester into its one-carbon homologue (RCO₂Et → RCH₂CO₂Et) through a lithium ynolate, with no diazomethane (compare the Arndt–Eistert synthesis). Acidic ethanol traps the ynolate as the ethyl ester.","mc":["Dibromomethyllithium (from CH₂Br₂ and LiTMP) adds to the ester; the adduct loses ethoxide and LiHMDS turns the dibromomethyl ketone into its enolate.","s-BuLi exchanges a bromine for lithium; the carbenoid loses LiBr and the phenyl group migrates (1,2-shift), giving a lithium ynolate Ph–C≡C–OLi.","Acidic ethanol protonates the ynolate to a ketene, which ethanol traps as the homologated ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kowalski ester homologation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kowalski_ester_homologation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kowalski ester homologation"},{"x":"O'Donnell amino acid synthesis"},{"x":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis"},{"x":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[426],"cond":["CH₂Br₂, LiTMP, THF, −78 °C","LiHMDS; then s-BuLi","HCl, EtOH"],"p":427},"ex":"Kowalski ester homologation: Turns an ester into its one-carbon homologue (RCO₂Et → RCH₂CO₂Et) through a lithium ynolate, with no diazomethane (compare the Arndt–Eistert synthesis). Acidic ethanol traps the ynolate as the ethyl ester.","mc":["Dibromomethyllithium (from CH₂Br₂ and LiTMP) adds to the ester; the adduct loses ethoxide and LiHMDS turns the dibromomethyl ketone into its enolate.","s-BuLi exchanges a bromine for lithium; the carbenoid loses LiBr and the phenyl group migrates (1,2-shift), giving a lithium ynolate Ph–C≡C–OLi.","Acidic ethanol protonates the ynolate to a ketene, which ethanol traps as the homologated ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kowalski ester homologation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kowalski_ester_homologation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":428},{"m":429},{"m":430},{"m":431}],"t":"Cannizzaro reaction","fig":{"r":[432],"cond":["NaOH (aq), heat","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"Strong base disproportionates an aldehyde without α-hydrogens: one carbonyl carbon is oxidised to a carboxylate and another reduced to an alcohol, by hydride transfer. Two benzaldehydes give benzoate and benzyl alcohol; an α-keto aldehyde does it intramolecularly, here giving mandelic acid. With formaldehyde as the hydride donor it is the crossed Cannizzaro reaction.","mc":["Hydroxide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral alkoxide.","The alkoxide pushes a hydride from that carbon onto the neighbouring ketone carbonyl.","This gives the carboxylate and the alkoxide; acid work-up gives mandelic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cannizzaro reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannizzaro_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Cannizzaro reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Seebach reaction"},{"x":"Aldol condensation"},{"x":"Malonic ester synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[432],"cond":["NaOH (aq), heat","H₃O⁺"],"p":433},"ex":"Cannizzaro reaction: Strong base disproportionates an aldehyde without α-hydrogens: one carbonyl carbon is oxidised to a carboxylate and another reduced to an alcohol, by hydride transfer. Two benzaldehydes give benzoate and benzyl alcohol; an α-keto aldehyde does it intramolecularly, here giving mandelic acid. With formaldehyde as the hydride donor it is the crossed Cannizzaro reaction.","mc":["Hydroxide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral alkoxide.","The alkoxide pushes a hydride from that carbon onto the neighbouring ketone carbonyl.","This gives the carboxylate and the alkoxide; acid work-up gives mandelic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cannizzaro reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannizzaro_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":434},{"m":282},{"m":435},{"m":436}],"t":"Tishchenko reaction","fig":{"r":[320,285],"cond":["Al(OEt)₃ (cat.)"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aluminium alkoxide disproportionates two aldehydes into an ester: one is oxidised to the acyl part, the other reduced to the alkoxy part. Like the Cannizzaro reaction but without water, so the ester forms instead of the acid and the alcohol. Ethyl acetate is made from acetaldehyde this way.","mc":["The aluminium alkoxide coordinates one aldehyde as a Lewis acid.","A second aldehyde adds, giving an aluminium hemiacetal alkoxide.","An intramolecular 1,3-hydride shift to the coordinated aldehyde carbon gives the ester and frees the catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tishchenko reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tishchenko_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Tishchenko reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Seebach reaction"},{"x":"Cannizzaro reaction"},{"x":"Baker–Venkataraman rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[320,285],"cond":["Al(OEt)₃ (cat.)"],"p":437},"ex":"Tishchenko reaction: An aluminium alkoxide disproportionates two aldehydes into an ester: one is oxidised to the acyl part, the other reduced to the alkoxy part. Like the Cannizzaro reaction but without water, so the ester forms instead of the acid and the alcohol. Ethyl acetate is made from acetaldehyde this way.","mc":["The aluminium alkoxide coordinates one aldehyde as a Lewis acid.","A second aldehyde adds, giving an aluminium hemiacetal alkoxide.","An intramolecular 1,3-hydride shift to the coordinated aldehyde carbon gives the ester and frees the catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tishchenko reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tishchenko_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":323},{"m":438},{"m":325},{"m":322}],"t":"Wittig reaction","fig":{"r":[263],"cond":["Ph₃P=CH₂ (from Ph₃P⁺CH₃ Br⁻ + n-BuLi), THF"],"veil":true},"ex":"A phosphonium ylide converts an aldehyde or ketone into an alkene. Unstabilised ylides favour Z-alkenes. The strong P=O bond of Ph₃PO drives the reaction.","mc":["n-BuLi deprotonates the phosphonium salt to the ylide Ph₃P=CH₂.","The ylide and the ketone combine in a [2+2] cycloaddition to an oxaphosphetane.","The oxaphosphetane breaks apart (retro-[2+2]) to the alkene and Ph₃P=O, driven by the strong P=O bond."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wittig reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wittig_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Wittig reaction"},{"x":"Cope elimination"},{"x":"Shapiro reaction"},{"x":"Hofmann elimination"}],"fig":{"r":[263],"cond":["Ph₃P=CH₂ (from Ph₃P⁺CH₃ Br⁻ + n-BuLi), THF"],"p":439},"ex":"Wittig reaction: A phosphonium ylide converts an aldehyde or ketone into an alkene. Unstabilised ylides favour Z-alkenes. The strong P=O bond of Ph₃PO drives the reaction.","mc":["n-BuLi deprotonates the phosphonium salt to the ylide Ph₃P=CH₂.","The ylide and the ketone combine in a [2+2] cycloaddition to an oxaphosphetane.","The oxaphosphetane breaks apart (retro-[2+2]) to the alkene and Ph₃P=O, driven by the strong P=O bond."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wittig reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wittig_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":440},{"m":441},{"m":442},{"m":443}],"t":"Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction","fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["(EtO)₂P(O)CH₂CO₂Et, NaH, THF"],"veil":true},"ex":"A phosphonate carbanion turns an aldehyde into an (E)-α,β-unsaturated ester. The phosphate by-product washes out with water, unlike Ph₃PO.","mc":["NaH deprotonates the phosphonate to a stabilised carbanion.","The carbanion adds reversibly to the aldehyde; equilibration favours the adduct leading to the E-alkene.","The alkoxide attacks phosphorus to form an oxaphosphetane, which eliminates a water-soluble phosphate to give the E-alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horner%E2%80%93Wadsworth%E2%80%93Emmons_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction"},{"x":"Shapiro reaction"},{"x":"Cross metathesis"},{"x":"Ring-opening metathesis"}],"fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["(EtO)₂P(O)CH₂CO₂Et, NaH, THF"],"p":444},"ex":"Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction: A phosphonate carbanion turns an aldehyde into an (E)-α,β-unsaturated ester. The phosphate by-product washes out with water, unlike Ph₃PO.","mc":["NaH deprotonates the phosphonate to a stabilised carbanion.","The carbanion adds reversibly to the aldehyde; equilibration favours the adduct leading to the E-alkene.","The alkoxide attacks phosphorus to form an oxaphosphetane, which eliminates a water-soluble phosphate to give the E-alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horner%E2%80%93Wadsworth%E2%80%93Emmons_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":445},{"m":446},{"m":447},{"m":448}],"t":"Still–Gennari olefination","fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["(CF₃CH₂O)₂P(O)CH₂CO₂Me, KHMDS, 18-crown-6, THF, −78 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons variant with trifluoroethyl phosphonates that gives (Z)-unsaturated esters. The electron-poor phosphonate makes the elimination fast, locking in the Z geometry.","mc":["KHMDS with 18-crown-6 gives a free, highly reactive phosphonate anion.","The anion adds to the aldehyde under kinetic control.","The electron-poor trifluoroethyl groups speed up oxaphosphetane formation and elimination, so the kinetic adduct gives the Z-alkene before it can equilibrate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Still–Gennari olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horner%E2%80%93Wadsworth%E2%80%93Emmons_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Still–Gennari olefination"},{"x":"Shapiro reaction"},{"x":"Wittig reaction"},{"x":"McMurry reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["(CF₃CH₂O)₂P(O)CH₂CO₂Me, KHMDS, 18-crown-6, THF, −78 °C"],"p":449},"ex":"Still–Gennari olefination: A Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons variant with trifluoroethyl phosphonates that gives (Z)-unsaturated esters. The electron-poor phosphonate makes the elimination fast, locking in the Z geometry.","mc":["KHMDS with 18-crown-6 gives a free, highly reactive phosphonate anion.","The anion adds to the aldehyde under kinetic control.","The electron-poor trifluoroethyl groups speed up oxaphosphetane formation and elimination, so the kinetic adduct gives the Z-alkene before it can equilibrate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Still–Gennari olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horner%E2%80%93Wadsworth%E2%80%93Emmons_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":450},{"m":451},{"m":452},{"m":453}],"t":"Julia–Lythgoe olefination","fig":{"r":[454,455],"cond":["n-BuLi, THF, −78 °C","Ac₂O","Na(Hg), MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"A phenyl sulfone carbanion adds to an aldehyde; the β-acetoxy sulfone is then reduced (Na/Hg or SmI₂) to the E-alkene. The reduction goes through a vinylic radical that can equilibrate, so the more stable E-alkene forms. The Julia–Kocienski version does it in one pot.","mc":["n-BuLi deprotonates the sulfone next to sulfur; the carbanion adds to the aldehyde, giving a β-alkoxy sulfone.","Acetic anhydride traps the alkoxide as the acetate, a better leaving group.","Sodium amalgam removes the sulfone; the reduction passes through a vinylic radical, which is reduced and protonated to the alkene, mainly E."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Julia–Lythgoe olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_olefination#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Julia–Lythgoe olefination"},{"x":"Bamford–Stevens reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Fuchs reaction"},{"x":"Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[454,455],"cond":["n-BuLi, THF, −78 °C","Ac₂O","Na(Hg), MeOH"],"p":456},"ex":"Julia–Lythgoe olefination: A phenyl sulfone carbanion adds to an aldehyde; the β-acetoxy sulfone is then reduced (Na/Hg or SmI₂) to the E-alkene. The reduction goes through a vinylic radical that can equilibrate, so the more stable E-alkene forms. The Julia–Kocienski version does it in one pot.","mc":["n-BuLi deprotonates the sulfone next to sulfur; the carbanion adds to the aldehyde, giving a β-alkoxy sulfone.","Acetic anhydride traps the alkoxide as the acetate, a better leaving group.","Sodium amalgam removes the sulfone; the reduction passes through a vinylic radical, which is reduced and protonated to the alkene, mainly E."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Julia–Lythgoe olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_olefination#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":450},{"m":457},{"m":458},{"m":459}],"t":"Julia–Kocienski olefination","fig":{"r":[454,460],"cond":["KHMDS, DME, −60 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A heteroaryl (tetrazolyl) sulfone carbanion adds to an aldehyde and eliminates in one pot to an E-alkene. A one-pot version of the original Julia–Lythgoe olefination.","mc":["KHMDS deprotonates the tetrazolyl sulfone next to sulfur.","The carbanion adds to the aldehyde, giving a β-alkoxy sulfone.","The alkoxide attacks the tetrazole carbon (an intramolecular aromatic substitution), moving the heteroaryl group from sulfur to oxygen.","The sulfinate eliminates SO₂ and the tetrazolone anion, giving mainly the E-alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Julia–Kocienski olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_olefination#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Julia–Kocienski olefination"},{"x":"McMurry reaction"},{"x":"Julia–Lythgoe olefination"},{"x":"Cope elimination"}],"fig":{"r":[454,460],"cond":["KHMDS, DME, −60 °C"],"p":456},"ex":"Julia–Kocienski olefination: A heteroaryl (tetrazolyl) sulfone carbanion adds to an aldehyde and eliminates in one pot to an E-alkene. A one-pot version of the original Julia–Lythgoe olefination.","mc":["KHMDS deprotonates the tetrazolyl sulfone next to sulfur.","The carbanion adds to the aldehyde, giving a β-alkoxy sulfone.","The alkoxide attacks the tetrazole carbon (an intramolecular aromatic substitution), moving the heteroaryl group from sulfur to oxygen.","The sulfinate eliminates SO₂ and the tetrazolone anion, giving mainly the E-alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Julia–Kocienski olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_olefination#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":461},{"m":462},{"m":463},{"m":464}],"t":"Peterson olefination","fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["Me₃SiCH₂Li, THF","KH (syn) or H₂SO₄ (anti)"],"veil":true},"ex":"An α-silyl carbanion adds to a carbonyl; the β-hydroxysilane then eliminates to the alkene. Acid and base eliminate with opposite stereochemistry (anti vs syn).","mc":["Me₃SiCH₂Li adds to the carbonyl, giving a β-oxidosilane (a β-hydroxysilane after work-up).","With base (KH) the alkoxide attacks silicon and Me₃SiO⁻ is eliminated syn, through a four-membered ring.","With acid the protonated OH leaves anti to the silyl group, giving the opposite alkene geometry."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Peterson olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peterson_olefination#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Peterson olefination"},{"x":"Julia–Kocienski olefination"},{"x":"Tebbe olefination"},{"x":"Shapiro reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["Me₃SiCH₂Li, THF","KH (syn) or H₂SO₄ (anti)"],"p":465},"ex":"Peterson olefination: An α-silyl carbanion adds to a carbonyl; the β-hydroxysilane then eliminates to the alkene. Acid and base eliminate with opposite stereochemistry (anti vs syn).","mc":["Me₃SiCH₂Li adds to the carbonyl, giving a β-oxidosilane (a β-hydroxysilane after work-up).","With base (KH) the alkoxide attacks silicon and Me₃SiO⁻ is eliminated syn, through a four-membered ring.","With acid the protonated OH leaves anti to the silyl group, giving the opposite alkene geometry."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Peterson olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peterson_olefination#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":466},{"m":467},{"m":468},{"m":469}],"t":"McMurry reaction","fig":{"r":[263,263],"cond":["TiCl₄, Zn (low-valent Ti), THF, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"Low-valent titanium reductively couples two ketones or aldehydes into an alkene. Works even for hindered, tetrasubstituted alkenes.","mc":["Zinc reduces TiCl₄ to low-valent titanium.","Single-electron transfer to each ketone gives ketyl radicals, which couple to a titanium pinacolate.","The titanium surface removes both oxygens from the pinacolate, forming the C=C bond and titanium oxides."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["McMurry reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McMurry_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"McMurry reaction"},{"x":"Julia–Kocienski olefination"},{"x":"Enyne metathesis"},{"x":"Wittig reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[263,263],"cond":["TiCl₄, Zn (low-valent Ti), THF, reflux"],"p":470},"ex":"McMurry reaction: Low-valent titanium reductively couples two ketones or aldehydes into an alkene. Works even for hindered, tetrasubstituted alkenes.","mc":["Zinc reduces TiCl₄ to low-valent titanium.","Single-electron transfer to each ketone gives ketyl radicals, which couple to a titanium pinacolate.","The titanium surface removes both oxygens from the pinacolate, forming the C=C bond and titanium oxides."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["McMurry reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McMurry_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":471},{"m":472},{"m":473},{"m":474}],"t":"Corey–Fuchs reaction","fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["CBr₄, PPh₃, CH₂Cl₂","n-BuLi (2 equiv), then H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Converts an aldehyde into a terminal alkyne via a 1,1-dibromoalkene. The intermediate lithium acetylide can be trapped with other electrophiles.","mc":["PPh₃ and CBr₄ form the ylide Ph₃P=CBr₂, which converts the aldehyde into a 1,1-dibromoalkene (Wittig).","The first n-BuLi does a lithium–halogen exchange; the vinyl carbenoid loses LiBr and rearranges by a 1,2-shift to the alkyne.","The second n-BuLi deprotonates the alkyne to the lithium acetylide, which water protonates."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Fuchs reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Fuchs_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Corey–Fuchs reaction"},{"x":"Julia–Lythgoe olefination"},{"x":"Ester pyrolysis"},{"x":"Seyferth–Gilbert homologation"}],"fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["CBr₄, PPh₃, CH₂Cl₂","n-BuLi (2 equiv), then H₂O"],"p":475},"ex":"Corey–Fuchs reaction: Converts an aldehyde into a terminal alkyne via a 1,1-dibromoalkene. The intermediate lithium acetylide can be trapped with other electrophiles.","mc":["PPh₃ and CBr₄ form the ylide Ph₃P=CBr₂, which converts the aldehyde into a 1,1-dibromoalkene (Wittig).","The first n-BuLi does a lithium–halogen exchange; the vinyl carbenoid loses LiBr and rearranges by a 1,2-shift to the alkyne.","The second n-BuLi deprotonates the alkyne to the lithium acetylide, which water protonates."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Fuchs reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Fuchs_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":476},{"m":477},{"m":478},{"m":479}],"t":"Seyferth–Gilbert homologation","fig":{"r":[285],"cond":["Ohira–Bestmann reagent, K₂CO₃, MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"A diazophosphonate turns an aldehyde into a terminal alkyne in one step. The Ohira–Bestmann reagent makes it mild enough for enolisable aldehydes.","mc":["Methoxide (K₂CO₃ in MeOH) removes the acetyl group of the Ohira–Bestmann reagent, releasing the diazomethylphosphonate anion.","The anion adds to the aldehyde; an oxaphosphetane forms and loses dimethyl phosphate, giving a vinyl diazo compound.","Loss of N₂ gives a vinylidene carbene, which undergoes a 1,2-hydrogen shift to the terminal alkyne."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Seyferth–Gilbert homologation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seyferth%E2%80%93Gilbert_homologation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Seyferth–Gilbert homologation"},{"x":"Julia–Kocienski olefination"},{"x":"Peterson olefination"},{"x":"Shapiro reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[285],"cond":["Ohira–Bestmann reagent, K₂CO₃, MeOH"],"p":480},"ex":"Seyferth–Gilbert homologation: A diazophosphonate turns an aldehyde into a terminal alkyne in one step. The Ohira–Bestmann reagent makes it mild enough for enolisable aldehydes.","mc":["Methoxide (K₂CO₃ in MeOH) removes the acetyl group of the Ohira–Bestmann reagent, releasing the diazomethylphosphonate anion.","The anion adds to the aldehyde; an oxaphosphetane forms and loses dimethyl phosphate, giving a vinyl diazo compound.","Loss of N₂ gives a vinylidene carbene, which undergoes a 1,2-hydrogen shift to the terminal alkyne."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Seyferth–Gilbert homologation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seyferth%E2%80%93Gilbert_homologation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":481},{"m":482},{"m":483},{"m":468}],"t":"Shapiro reaction","fig":{"r":[484],"cond":["TsNHNH₂","n-BuLi (2 equiv)","H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"A ketone tosylhydrazone and excess alkyllithium give an alkene via a vinyllithium. Gives the less substituted alkene, unlike the Bamford–Stevens reaction.","mc":["The ketone condenses with tosylhydrazine to a tosylhydrazone.","Two equivalents of n-BuLi remove the N–H and then the less hindered α-C–H, giving a dianion.","Loss of the sulfinate and then N₂ gives a vinyllithium.","Water protonates the vinyllithium to the alkene; other electrophiles can be used instead."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Shapiro reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shapiro_reaction#Reaction_mechanism_and_directionality"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Shapiro reaction"},{"x":"Bamford–Stevens reaction"},{"x":"Ring-closing metathesis"},{"x":"Ester pyrolysis"}],"fig":{"r":[484],"cond":["TsNHNH₂","n-BuLi (2 equiv)","H₂O"],"p":485},"ex":"Shapiro reaction: A ketone tosylhydrazone and excess alkyllithium give an alkene via a vinyllithium. Gives the less substituted alkene, unlike the Bamford–Stevens reaction.","mc":["The ketone condenses with tosylhydrazine to a tosylhydrazone.","Two equivalents of n-BuLi remove the N–H and then the less hindered α-C–H, giving a dianion.","Loss of the sulfinate and then N₂ gives a vinyllithium.","Water protonates the vinyllithium to the alkene; other electrophiles can be used instead."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Shapiro reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shapiro_reaction#Reaction_mechanism_and_directionality"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":486},{"m":487},{"m":488},{"m":489}],"t":"Bamford–Stevens reaction","fig":{"r":[490],"cond":["TsNHNH₂","NaOMe, diglyme, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A tosylhydrazone and a strong base give a diazo compound, which loses N₂ on heating to an alkene. In aprotic solvents the diazo compound gives a carbene, in protic ones a carbocation, so mixtures and rearrangements are common; here both α-carbons are the same. With alkyllithiums (Shapiro) a vinyllithium forms instead.","mc":["The ketone condenses with tosylhydrazine to a tosylhydrazone.","Methoxide removes the N–H; the anion loses toluenesulfinate, giving a diazo compound.","On heating the diazo compound loses N₂, giving a carbene (in protic solvents a carbocation instead).","A 1,2-hydrogen shift from the neighbouring CH₂ gives the alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bamford–Stevens reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamford%E2%80%93Stevens_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Bamford–Stevens reaction"},{"x":"McMurry reaction"},{"x":"Wittig reaction"},{"x":"Peterson olefination"}],"fig":{"r":[490],"cond":["TsNHNH₂","NaOMe, diglyme, heat"],"p":491},"ex":"Bamford–Stevens reaction: A tosylhydrazone and a strong base give a diazo compound, which loses N₂ on heating to an alkene. In aprotic solvents the diazo compound gives a carbene, in protic ones a carbocation, so mixtures and rearrangements are common; here both α-carbons are the same. With alkyllithiums (Shapiro) a vinyllithium forms instead.","mc":["The ketone condenses with tosylhydrazine to a tosylhydrazone.","Methoxide removes the N–H; the anion loses toluenesulfinate, giving a diazo compound.","On heating the diazo compound loses N₂, giving a carbene (in protic solvents a carbocation instead).","A 1,2-hydrogen shift from the neighbouring CH₂ gives the alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bamford–Stevens reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamford%E2%80%93Stevens_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":492},{"m":493},{"m":494},{"m":495}],"t":"Ring-closing metathesis","fig":{"r":[496],"cond":["Grubbs II (cat.), CH₂Cl₂, 40 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A metal carbene catalyst swaps alkene partners; two terminal alkenes close a ring and release ethylene. Loss of volatile ethylene drives the equilibrium.","mc":["The Ru carbene catalyst and one terminal alkene form a ruthenacyclobutane by [2+2] cycloaddition.","Retro-[2+2] gives a new Ru alkylidene on the substrate chain.","The tethered second alkene reacts with it intramolecularly through another ruthenacyclobutane.","Retro-[2+2] releases the cycloalkene and a Ru methylidene, which continues the cycle; ethylene is lost overall (Chauvin mechanism)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ring-closing metathesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring-closing_metathesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ring-closing metathesis"},{"x":"Cope elimination"},{"x":"Ester pyrolysis"},{"x":"Still–Gennari olefination"}],"fig":{"r":[496],"cond":["Grubbs II (cat.), CH₂Cl₂, 40 °C"],"p":497},"ex":"Ring-closing metathesis: A metal carbene catalyst swaps alkene partners; two terminal alkenes close a ring and release ethylene. Loss of volatile ethylene drives the equilibrium.","mc":["The Ru carbene catalyst and one terminal alkene form a ruthenacyclobutane by [2+2] cycloaddition.","Retro-[2+2] gives a new Ru alkylidene on the substrate chain.","The tethered second alkene reacts with it intramolecularly through another ruthenacyclobutane.","Retro-[2+2] releases the cycloalkene and a Ru methylidene, which continues the cycle; ethylene is lost overall (Chauvin mechanism)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ring-closing metathesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring-closing_metathesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":498},{"m":499},{"m":500},{"m":501}],"t":"Hofmann elimination","fig":{"r":[502],"cond":["MeI (excess)","Ag₂O, H₂O, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A quaternary ammonium hydroxide eliminates on heating to give the less substituted alkene. The bulky leaving group makes the E2 favour the least hindered proton.","mc":["Excess MeI methylates the amine to the quaternary ammonium iodide.","Ag₂O in water exchanges iodide for hydroxide.","On heating, hydroxide removes a β-hydrogen (E2); the bulky NMe₃⁺ group directs it to the least hindered hydrogen (Hofmann rule)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hofmann elimination","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hofmann_elimination"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hofmann elimination"},{"x":"Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction"},{"x":"Wittig reaction"},{"x":"Bamford–Stevens reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[502],"cond":["MeI (excess)","Ag₂O, H₂O, heat"],"p":503},"ex":"Hofmann elimination: A quaternary ammonium hydroxide eliminates on heating to give the less substituted alkene. The bulky leaving group makes the E2 favour the least hindered proton.","mc":["Excess MeI methylates the amine to the quaternary ammonium iodide.","Ag₂O in water exchanges iodide for hydroxide.","On heating, hydroxide removes a β-hydrogen (E2); the bulky NMe₃⁺ group directs it to the least hindered hydrogen (Hofmann rule)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hofmann elimination","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hofmann_elimination"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":504},{"m":505},{"m":506},{"m":507}],"t":"Cope elimination","fig":{"r":[508],"cond":["H₂O₂ (or mCPBA)","heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A tertiary amine N-oxide undergoes syn-elimination on heating to an alkene and a hydroxylamine. Intramolecular, through a five-membered cyclic transition state.","mc":["H₂O₂ oxidises the tertiary amine to its N-oxide.","On heating, the N-oxide oxygen removes a syn β-hydrogen through a five-membered cyclic transition state (Ei).","The alkene and N,N-dimethylhydroxylamine form in this single concerted step."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cope elimination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cope_reaction#Mechanism_and_related_eliminations"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Cope elimination"},{"x":"Cross metathesis"},{"x":"Shapiro reaction"},{"x":"Hofmann elimination"}],"fig":{"r":[508],"cond":["H₂O₂ (or mCPBA)","heat"],"p":509},"ex":"Cope elimination: A tertiary amine N-oxide undergoes syn-elimination on heating to an alkene and a hydroxylamine. Intramolecular, through a five-membered cyclic transition state.","mc":["H₂O₂ oxidises the tertiary amine to its N-oxide.","On heating, the N-oxide oxygen removes a syn β-hydrogen through a five-membered cyclic transition state (Ei).","The alkene and N,N-dimethylhydroxylamine form in this single concerted step."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cope elimination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cope_reaction#Mechanism_and_related_eliminations"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":510},{"m":511},{"m":512},{"m":513}],"t":"Cross metathesis","fig":{"r":[514,515],"cond":["Grubbs II (5 mol%), CH₂Cl₂, 40 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A ruthenium carbene exchanges the alkylidene ends of two different terminal alkenes, joining them with loss of ethylene. Two terminal alkenes could give six products (two homodimers and the cross product, each E or Z); pairing a reactive alkene with a sluggish one such as an acrylate gives the E cross product selectively.","mc":["The ruthenium carbene and allylbenzene form a ruthenacyclobutane ([2+2]), which opens to a new carbene and ethylene.","This carbene undergoes [2+2] cycloaddition with methyl acrylate.","Retro-[2+2] releases the E cross product and a ruthenium methylidene, which carries on the cycle."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cross metathesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olefin_metathesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Cross metathesis"},{"x":"Wittig reaction"},{"x":"Julia–Lythgoe olefination"},{"x":"Ester pyrolysis"}],"fig":{"r":[514,515],"cond":["Grubbs II (5 mol%), CH₂Cl₂, 40 °C"],"p":516},"ex":"Cross metathesis: A ruthenium carbene exchanges the alkylidene ends of two different terminal alkenes, joining them with loss of ethylene. Two terminal alkenes could give six products (two homodimers and the cross product, each E or Z); pairing a reactive alkene with a sluggish one such as an acrylate gives the E cross product selectively.","mc":["The ruthenium carbene and allylbenzene form a ruthenacyclobutane ([2+2]), which opens to a new carbene and ethylene.","This carbene undergoes [2+2] cycloaddition with methyl acrylate.","Retro-[2+2] releases the E cross product and a ruthenium methylidene, which carries on the cycle."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cross metathesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olefin_metathesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":517},{"m":518},{"m":519},{"m":520}],"t":"Enyne metathesis","fig":{"r":[521],"cond":["Grubbs II (5 mol%), CH₂Cl₂, 40 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A ruthenium carbene joins an alkyne and an alkene into a 1,3-diene; intramolecularly it closes a ring that carries a vinyl group. No atoms are lost: the alkene's terminal CH₂ ends up on the alkyne carbon. Ethylene is often added to keep the catalyst turning over.","mc":["The ruthenium carbene reacts with the alkene ([2+2], then retro-[2+2]) and moves onto the substrate.","The new carbene adds to the alkyne intramolecularly, giving a ruthenacyclobutene that opens to a vinyl carbene.","This carbene reacts with another alkene, releasing the 1,3-diene and passing the ruthenium on."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Enyne metathesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enyne_metathesis#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Enyne metathesis"},{"x":"Ring-opening metathesis"},{"x":"Wittig reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Fuchs reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[521],"cond":["Grubbs II (5 mol%), CH₂Cl₂, 40 °C"],"p":522},"ex":"Enyne metathesis: A ruthenium carbene joins an alkyne and an alkene into a 1,3-diene; intramolecularly it closes a ring that carries a vinyl group. No atoms are lost: the alkene's terminal CH₂ ends up on the alkyne carbon. Ethylene is often added to keep the catalyst turning over.","mc":["The ruthenium carbene reacts with the alkene ([2+2], then retro-[2+2]) and moves onto the substrate.","The new carbene adds to the alkyne intramolecularly, giving a ruthenacyclobutene that opens to a vinyl carbene.","This carbene reacts with another alkene, releasing the 1,3-diene and passing the ruthenium on."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Enyne metathesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enyne_metathesis#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":523},{"m":524},{"m":525},{"m":526}],"t":"Ring-opening metathesis","fig":{"r":[527],"cond":["Grubbs I (cat.), ethylene (excess), CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"A ruthenium carbene opens a strained cyclic alkene; with an excess of a partner alkene (here ethylene) it gives a diene, and without one it polymerises (ROMP). Relief of ring strain drives it (norbornene, cyclobutenes, cyclooctene). It is the reverse of ring-closing metathesis, which wins for unstrained rings.","mc":["The ruthenium carbene adds to the strained norbornene double bond ([2+2]).","Retro-[2+2] opens the ring, releasing the strain and leaving a new carbene on the chain.","The carbene reacts with ethylene, releasing cis-1,3-divinylcyclopentane and a ruthenium methylidene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ring-opening metathesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring-opening_metathesis_polymerization#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ring-opening metathesis"},{"x":"Seyferth–Gilbert homologation"},{"x":"Shapiro reaction"},{"x":"Ester pyrolysis"}],"fig":{"r":[527],"cond":["Grubbs I (cat.), ethylene (excess), CH₂Cl₂"],"p":528},"ex":"Ring-opening metathesis: A ruthenium carbene opens a strained cyclic alkene; with an excess of a partner alkene (here ethylene) it gives a diene, and without one it polymerises (ROMP). Relief of ring strain drives it (norbornene, cyclobutenes, cyclooctene). It is the reverse of ring-closing metathesis, which wins for unstrained rings.","mc":["The ruthenium carbene adds to the strained norbornene double bond ([2+2]).","Retro-[2+2] opens the ring, releasing the strain and leaving a new carbene on the chain.","The carbene reacts with ethylene, releasing cis-1,3-divinylcyclopentane and a ruthenium methylidene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ring-opening metathesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ring-opening_metathesis_polymerization#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":529},{"m":530},{"m":477},{"m":531}],"t":"Schlosser modification","fig":{"r":[285],"cond":["Ph₃P⁺CH₂CH₃ Br⁻, PhLi, THF, −78 °C","PhCHO","PhLi; then t-BuOH","KOt-Bu"],"veil":true},"ex":"A Wittig reaction of a non-stabilised ylide made E-selective: the lithium betaine is deprotonated and reprotonated to its more stable isomer before it eliminates. Unmodified, non-stabilised ylides give mainly Z-alkenes. Lithium keeps the betaine from eliminating; potassium (KOt-Bu) then lets it release the E-alkene and Ph₃PO.","mc":["The ylide adds to benzaldehyde at −78 °C; lithium holds the adduct as a betaine instead of letting it close.","A second equivalent of PhLi deprotonates the betaine next to phosphorus (a β-oxido ylide); t-BuOH reprotonates it to the more stable threo betaine.","KOt-Bu exchanges lithium for potassium, so the oxaphosphetane forms and collapses to the E-alkene and Ph₃PO."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Schlosser modification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schlosser_modification#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Schlosser modification"},{"x":"Peterson olefination"},{"x":"Tebbe olefination"},{"x":"Enyne metathesis"}],"fig":{"r":[285],"cond":["Ph₃P⁺CH₂CH₃ Br⁻, PhLi, THF, −78 °C","PhCHO","PhLi; then t-BuOH","KOt-Bu"],"p":532},"ex":"Schlosser modification: A Wittig reaction of a non-stabilised ylide made E-selective: the lithium betaine is deprotonated and reprotonated to its more stable isomer before it eliminates. Unmodified, non-stabilised ylides give mainly Z-alkenes. Lithium keeps the betaine from eliminating; potassium (KOt-Bu) then lets it release the E-alkene and Ph₃PO.","mc":["The ylide adds to benzaldehyde at −78 °C; lithium holds the adduct as a betaine instead of letting it close.","A second equivalent of PhLi deprotonates the betaine next to phosphorus (a β-oxido ylide); t-BuOH reprotonates it to the more stable threo betaine.","KOt-Bu exchanges lithium for potassium, so the oxaphosphetane forms and collapses to the E-alkene and Ph₃PO."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Schlosser modification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schlosser_modification#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":533},{"m":534},{"m":424},{"m":535}],"t":"Ester pyrolysis","fig":{"r":[536],"cond":["heat (about 450 °C)"],"veil":true},"ex":"Heating an acetate ester eliminates acetic acid through a six-membered cyclic transition state (Ei), giving an alkene by syn elimination. It needs a β-hydrogen on the same side as the ester, and tends to give the less substituted alkene (Hofmann's rule). Amine oxides (Cope elimination) and xanthates react the same way at lower temperature.","mc":["On strong heating the carbonyl oxygen of the acetate reaches a β-hydrogen on the same side.","In a six-membered cyclic transition state the C–H and C–O bonds break as O–H and C=C form (Ei, syn).","This releases acetic acid and the alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ester pyrolysis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ester_pyrolysis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ester pyrolysis"},{"x":"Ring-closing metathesis"},{"x":"Schlosser modification"},{"x":"Shapiro reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[536],"cond":["heat (about 450 °C)"],"p":537},"ex":"Ester pyrolysis: Heating an acetate ester eliminates acetic acid through a six-membered cyclic transition state (Ei), giving an alkene by syn elimination. It needs a β-hydrogen on the same side as the ester, and tends to give the less substituted alkene (Hofmann's rule). Amine oxides (Cope elimination) and xanthates react the same way at lower temperature.","mc":["On strong heating the carbonyl oxygen of the acetate reaches a β-hydrogen on the same side.","In a six-membered cyclic transition state the C–H and C–O bonds break as O–H and C=C form (Ei, syn).","This releases acetic acid and the alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ester pyrolysis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ester_pyrolysis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":538},{"m":539},{"m":540},{"m":541}],"t":"Tebbe olefination","fig":{"r":[542],"cond":["Tebbe reagent, pyridine (cat.), THF, −40 °C → rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"The Tebbe reagent methylenates carbonyl groups (C=O → C=CH₂): aldehydes and ketones, and also esters, lactones and amides. Esters and lactones give enol ethers and amides enamines, which Wittig reagents do not touch; it is not basic, so enolisable ketones are not epimerised. Pyridine helps release Cp₂Ti=CH₂.","mc":["Pyridine takes Me₂AlCl from the Tebbe reagent, releasing the titanium methylidene Cp₂Ti=CH₂.","Cp₂Ti=CH₂ and the lactone carbonyl combine in a [2+2] cycloaddition to an oxatitanacyclobutane.","Retro-[2+2] gives the exocyclic enol ether and Cp₂Ti=O."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tebbe olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tebbe's_reagent#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Tebbe olefination"},{"x":"Seyferth–Gilbert homologation"},{"x":"Schlosser modification"},{"x":"Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[542],"cond":["Tebbe reagent, pyridine (cat.), THF, −40 °C → rt"],"p":543},"ex":"Tebbe olefination: The Tebbe reagent methylenates carbonyl groups (C=O → C=CH₂): aldehydes and ketones, and also esters, lactones and amides. Esters and lactones give enol ethers and amides enamines, which Wittig reagents do not touch; it is not basic, so enolisable ketones are not epimerised. Pyridine helps release Cp₂Ti=CH₂.","mc":["Pyridine takes Me₂AlCl from the Tebbe reagent, releasing the titanium methylidene Cp₂Ti=CH₂.","Cp₂Ti=CH₂ and the lactone carbonyl combine in a [2+2] cycloaddition to an oxatitanacyclobutane.","Retro-[2+2] gives the exocyclic enol ether and Cp₂Ti=O."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tebbe olefination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tebbe's_reagent#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":544},{"m":545},{"m":546},{"m":547}],"t":"Glaser coupling","fig":{"r":[548,548],"cond":["CuCl (cat.), NH₄OH/EtOH, O₂ (air)"],"veil":true},"ex":"Terminal alkynes couple oxidatively with copper(I) and oxygen to a symmetrical 1,3-diyne. Oxygen reoxidises the copper, so a catalytic amount is enough. Two different alkynes give a statistical mixture, so it suits symmetrical and cyclic diynes.","mc":["Copper(I) and base turn the terminal alkyne into a copper acetylide.","Two acetylide units couple oxidatively at copper, giving the 1,3-diyne and reduced copper.","Oxygen reoxidises the copper, so only a catalytic amount is needed."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Glaser coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaser_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Glaser coupling"},{"x":"Myers–Saito cyclization"},{"x":"Eglinton reaction"},{"x":"Bergman cyclization"}],"fig":{"r":[548,548],"cond":["CuCl (cat.), NH₄OH/EtOH, O₂ (air)"],"p":549},"ex":"Glaser coupling: Terminal alkynes couple oxidatively with copper(I) and oxygen to a symmetrical 1,3-diyne. Oxygen reoxidises the copper, so a catalytic amount is enough. Two different alkynes give a statistical mixture, so it suits symmetrical and cyclic diynes.","mc":["Copper(I) and base turn the terminal alkyne into a copper acetylide.","Two acetylide units couple oxidatively at copper, giving the 1,3-diyne and reduced copper.","Oxygen reoxidises the copper, so only a catalytic amount is needed."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Glaser coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaser_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":550},{"m":551},{"m":552},{"m":553}],"t":"Hay coupling","fig":{"r":[554,554],"cond":["CuCl·TMEDA (cat.), O₂, acetone, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Copper(I)–TMEDA and oxygen couple terminal alkynes to symmetrical 1,3-diynes. The TMEDA complex dissolves in many more solvents than the Glaser system, which makes the coupling more versatile.","mc":["TMEDA-bound copper(I) and the alkyne form a copper acetylide.","Two acetylides couple oxidatively at copper, giving the diyne.","Oxygen reoxidises the copper–TMEDA catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Glaser coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaser_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hay coupling"},{"x":"Cadiot–Chodkiewicz coupling"},{"x":"Bergman cyclization"},{"x":"Glaser coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[554,554],"cond":["CuCl·TMEDA (cat.), O₂, acetone, rt"],"p":555},"ex":"Hay coupling: Copper(I)–TMEDA and oxygen couple terminal alkynes to symmetrical 1,3-diynes. The TMEDA complex dissolves in many more solvents than the Glaser system, which makes the coupling more versatile.","mc":["TMEDA-bound copper(I) and the alkyne form a copper acetylide.","Two acetylides couple oxidatively at copper, giving the diyne.","Oxygen reoxidises the copper–TMEDA catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Glaser coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaser_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":556},{"m":557},{"m":558},{"m":559}],"t":"Eglinton reaction","fig":{"r":[560,560],"cond":["Cu(OAc)₂ (stoichiometric), pyridine/MeOH, 60 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A stoichiometric copper(II) salt in pyridine couples terminal alkynes to symmetrical or cyclic 1,3-diynes. Copper(II) is the oxidant, so no oxygen is needed; at high dilution α,ω-diynes close macrocycles.","mc":["Pyridine removes the alkyne proton as copper(II) binds, giving a copper acetylide.","Two acetylide units couple, each copper(II) taking up one electron.","The diyne is released along with copper(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Glaser coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaser_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Eglinton reaction"},{"x":"Glaser coupling"},{"x":"Myers–Saito cyclization"},{"x":"Hay coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[560,560],"cond":["Cu(OAc)₂ (stoichiometric), pyridine/MeOH, 60 °C"],"p":561},"ex":"Eglinton reaction: A stoichiometric copper(II) salt in pyridine couples terminal alkynes to symmetrical or cyclic 1,3-diynes. Copper(II) is the oxidant, so no oxygen is needed; at high dilution α,ω-diynes close macrocycles.","mc":["Pyridine removes the alkyne proton as copper(II) binds, giving a copper acetylide.","Two acetylide units couple, each copper(II) taking up one electron.","The diyne is released along with copper(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Glaser coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaser_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":562},{"m":563},{"m":564},{"m":565}],"t":"Cadiot–Chodkiewicz coupling","fig":{"r":[566,567],"cond":["CuCl (cat.), NH₂OH·HCl, EtNH₂, MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"Copper(I) couples a terminal alkyne with a 1-bromoalkyne, giving an unsymmetrical 1,3-diyne. Hydroxylamine keeps the copper as Cu(I) and the amine forms the acetylide. Oxidative (Glaser-type) couplings give only symmetrical diynes.","mc":["The amine and copper(I) turn the terminal alkyne into a copper acetylide.","The bromoalkyne adds oxidatively to the copper acetylide.","Reductive elimination joins the two alkynes, giving the diyne and copper(I) bromide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cadiot–Chodkiewicz coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadiot%E2%80%93Chodkiewicz_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Cadiot–Chodkiewicz coupling"},{"x":"Myers–Saito cyclization"},{"x":"Bergman cyclization"},{"x":"Hay coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[566,567],"cond":["CuCl (cat.), NH₂OH·HCl, EtNH₂, MeOH"],"p":568},"ex":"Cadiot–Chodkiewicz coupling: Copper(I) couples a terminal alkyne with a 1-bromoalkyne, giving an unsymmetrical 1,3-diyne. Hydroxylamine keeps the copper as Cu(I) and the amine forms the acetylide. Oxidative (Glaser-type) couplings give only symmetrical diynes.","mc":["The amine and copper(I) turn the terminal alkyne into a copper acetylide.","The bromoalkyne adds oxidatively to the copper acetylide.","Reductive elimination joins the two alkynes, giving the diyne and copper(I) bromide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cadiot–Chodkiewicz coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cadiot%E2%80%93Chodkiewicz_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":569},{"m":570},{"m":571},{"m":572}],"t":"Bergman cyclization","fig":{"r":[573],"cond":["1,4-cyclohexadiene (H donor), 200 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"An enediyne cyclises on heating (or with light) to a 1,4-benzenediyl (p-benzyne) diradical, which abstracts two hydrogen atoms from a donor, giving an arene. Most enediynes need about 200 °C; ring strain lowers the barrier. Natural enediynes such as calicheamicin cyclise in cells, and the diradical cuts both strands of DNA.","mc":["On heating the two alkyne ends bond, closing a six-membered ring.","This gives a 1,4-benzenediyl diradical (a p-benzyne), with the two radical centres para to each other.","Each radical abstracts a hydrogen atom from 1,4-cyclohexadiene, giving the arene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bergman cyclization","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bergman_cyclization"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Bergman cyclization"},{"x":"Cadiot–Chodkiewicz coupling"},{"x":"Glaser coupling"},{"x":"Eglinton reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[573],"cond":["1,4-cyclohexadiene (H donor), 200 °C"],"p":574},"ex":"Bergman cyclization: An enediyne cyclises on heating (or with light) to a 1,4-benzenediyl (p-benzyne) diradical, which abstracts two hydrogen atoms from a donor, giving an arene. Most enediynes need about 200 °C; ring strain lowers the barrier. Natural enediynes such as calicheamicin cyclise in cells, and the diradical cuts both strands of DNA.","mc":["On heating the two alkyne ends bond, closing a six-membered ring.","This gives a 1,4-benzenediyl diradical (a p-benzyne), with the two radical centres para to each other.","Each radical abstracts a hydrogen atom from 1,4-cyclohexadiene, giving the arene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bergman cyclization","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bergman_cyclization"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":575},{"m":576},{"m":577},{"m":578}],"t":"Myers–Saito cyclization","fig":{"r":[579],"cond":["1,4-cyclohexadiene (H donor), 37 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"An enyne-allene cyclises (C2–C7) to an α,3-didehydrotoluene diradical, which abstracts two hydrogen atoms, giving a toluene. Much faster than the Bergman cyclisation: the parent compound cyclises at body temperature. The neocarzinostatin chromophore is activated this way.","mc":["The central allene carbon bonds to the alkyne end (C2–C7), closing a six-membered ring.","This gives the α,3-didehydrotoluene diradical: a benzyl radical and a σ aryl radical.","Both radicals abstract hydrogen atoms from 1,4-cyclohexadiene, giving toluene."],"lk":[{"h":"Organic Chemistry Portal","l":[["Myers–Saito cyclization","https://www.organic-chemistry.org/namedreactions/bergman-cyclization.shtm"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Myers–Saito cyclization"},{"x":"Hay coupling"},{"x":"Cadiot–Chodkiewicz coupling"},{"x":"Eglinton reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[579],"cond":["1,4-cyclohexadiene (H donor), 37 °C"],"p":580},"ex":"Myers–Saito cyclization: An enyne-allene cyclises (C2–C7) to an α,3-didehydrotoluene diradical, which abstracts two hydrogen atoms, giving a toluene. Much faster than the Bergman cyclisation: the parent compound cyclises at body temperature. The neocarzinostatin chromophore is activated this way.","mc":["The central allene carbon bonds to the alkyne end (C2–C7), closing a six-membered ring.","This gives the α,3-didehydrotoluene diradical: a benzyl radical and a σ aryl radical.","Both radicals abstract hydrogen atoms from 1,4-cyclohexadiene, giving toluene."],"lk":[{"h":"Organic Chemistry Portal","l":[["Myers–Saito cyclization","https://www.organic-chemistry.org/namedreactions/bergman-cyclization.shtm"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":581},{"m":582},{"m":583},{"m":584}],"t":"Suzuki–Miyaura coupling","fig":{"r":[585,586],"cond":["Pd(PPh₃)₄ (cat.), K₂CO₃, dioxane/H₂O, 90 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd-catalysed coupling of an organoboron compound with an aryl or vinyl halide. Needs a base to activate the boron. Boronic acids are stable and low in toxicity.","mc":["Oxidative addition: Pd(0) inserts into the Ar–Br bond, giving Ar–Pd(II)–Br.","Base activates boron (forming a boronate or a Pd–OH species), and the phenyl group transmetallates from boron to palladium.","Reductive elimination joins the two aryl groups and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Suzuki–Miyaura coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suzuki_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Suzuki–Miyaura coupling"},{"x":"Kumada coupling"},{"x":"Rosenmund–von Braun reaction"},{"x":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination"}],"fig":{"r":[585,586],"cond":["Pd(PPh₃)₄ (cat.), K₂CO₃, dioxane/H₂O, 90 °C"],"p":587},"ex":"Suzuki–Miyaura coupling: Pd-catalysed coupling of an organoboron compound with an aryl or vinyl halide. Needs a base to activate the boron. Boronic acids are stable and low in toxicity.","mc":["Oxidative addition: Pd(0) inserts into the Ar–Br bond, giving Ar–Pd(II)–Br.","Base activates boron (forming a boronate or a Pd–OH species), and the phenyl group transmetallates from boron to palladium.","Reductive elimination joins the two aryl groups and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Suzuki–Miyaura coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suzuki_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":588},{"m":589},{"m":590},{"m":591}],"t":"Heck reaction","fig":{"r":[592,308],"cond":["Pd(OAc)₂ (cat.), PPh₃, Et₃N, 100 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd-catalysed coupling of an aryl or vinyl halide with an alkene, giving a substituted alkene. Ends with β-hydride elimination, which usually gives the E-alkene.","mc":["Oxidative addition of PhBr to Pd(0) gives Ph–Pd–Br.","The acrylate coordinates and inserts into the Pd–Ph bond (syn), phenyl going to the less hindered carbon.","Syn β-hydride elimination releases the E-cinnamate and H–Pd–Br.","Et₃N removes HBr from H–Pd–Br, regenerating Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Heck reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heck_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Heck reaction"},{"x":"Fukuyama coupling"},{"x":"Hiyama–Denmark coupling"},{"x":"Kumada coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[592,308],"cond":["Pd(OAc)₂ (cat.), PPh₃, Et₃N, 100 °C"],"p":593},"ex":"Heck reaction: Pd-catalysed coupling of an aryl or vinyl halide with an alkene, giving a substituted alkene. Ends with β-hydride elimination, which usually gives the E-alkene.","mc":["Oxidative addition of PhBr to Pd(0) gives Ph–Pd–Br.","The acrylate coordinates and inserts into the Pd–Ph bond (syn), phenyl going to the less hindered carbon.","Syn β-hydride elimination releases the E-cinnamate and H–Pd–Br.","Et₃N removes HBr from H–Pd–Br, regenerating Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Heck reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heck_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":594},{"m":595},{"m":596},{"m":597}],"t":"Sonogashira coupling","fig":{"r":[598,599],"cond":["PdCl₂(PPh₃)₂, CuI (cat.), Et₃N"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd/Cu-catalysed coupling of a terminal alkyne with an aryl or vinyl halide. Copper forms the acetylide that transmetallates to palladium.","mc":["Oxidative addition of PhI to Pd(0).","CuI and Et₃N convert the terminal alkyne into a copper acetylide.","The alkynyl group transmetallates from copper to palladium.","Reductive elimination forms the C(sp²)–C(sp) bond and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sonogashira coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonogashira_coupling#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Sonogashira coupling"},{"x":"Fukuyama coupling"},{"x":"Rosenmund–von Braun reaction"},{"x":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination"}],"fig":{"r":[598,599],"cond":["PdCl₂(PPh₃)₂, CuI (cat.), Et₃N"],"p":600},"ex":"Sonogashira coupling: Pd/Cu-catalysed coupling of a terminal alkyne with an aryl or vinyl halide. Copper forms the acetylide that transmetallates to palladium.","mc":["Oxidative addition of PhI to Pd(0).","CuI and Et₃N convert the terminal alkyne into a copper acetylide.","The alkynyl group transmetallates from copper to palladium.","Reductive elimination forms the C(sp²)–C(sp) bond and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sonogashira coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonogashira_coupling#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":577},{"m":601},{"m":602},{"m":603}],"t":"Stille coupling","fig":{"r":[592,604],"cond":["Pd(PPh₃)₄ (cat.), toluene, 110 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd-catalysed coupling of an organostannane with an organic halide or triflate. Tolerates almost any functional group, but tin compounds are toxic.","mc":["Oxidative addition of PhBr to Pd(0).","Transmetallation moves the vinyl group from tin to palladium (vinyl transfers much faster than butyl), releasing Bu₃SnBr.","Reductive elimination gives styrene and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Stille coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stille_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Stille coupling"},{"x":"Tsuji–Trost reaction"},{"x":"Sonogashira coupling"},{"x":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination"}],"fig":{"r":[592,604],"cond":["Pd(PPh₃)₄ (cat.), toluene, 110 °C"],"p":605},"ex":"Stille coupling: Pd-catalysed coupling of an organostannane with an organic halide or triflate. Tolerates almost any functional group, but tin compounds are toxic.","mc":["Oxidative addition of PhBr to Pd(0).","Transmetallation moves the vinyl group from tin to palladium (vinyl transfers much faster than butyl), releasing Bu₃SnBr.","Reductive elimination gives styrene and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Stille coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stille_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":606},{"m":607},{"m":608},{"m":609}],"t":"Negishi coupling","fig":{"r":[592,610],"cond":["Pd(PPh₃)₄ (cat.), THF"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd- or Ni-catalysed coupling of an organozinc reagent with an organic halide. Organozincs are reactive yet tolerate esters and nitriles.","mc":["Oxidative addition of PhBr to Pd(0).","The ethyl group transmetallates from zinc to palladium.","Reductive elimination gives ethylbenzene and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Negishi coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negishi_coupling#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Negishi coupling"},{"x":"Ullmann condensation"},{"x":"Kumada coupling"},{"x":"Rosenmund–von Braun reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[592,610],"cond":["Pd(PPh₃)₄ (cat.), THF"],"p":611},"ex":"Negishi coupling: Pd- or Ni-catalysed coupling of an organozinc reagent with an organic halide. Organozincs are reactive yet tolerate esters and nitriles.","mc":["Oxidative addition of PhBr to Pd(0).","The ethyl group transmetallates from zinc to palladium.","Reductive elimination gives ethylbenzene and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Negishi coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negishi_coupling#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":612},{"m":607},{"m":613},{"m":614}],"t":"Kumada coupling","fig":{"r":[592,615],"cond":["NiCl₂(dppp) (cat.), Et₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Ni- or Pd-catalysed coupling of a Grignard reagent with an aryl or vinyl halide. The first catalytic cross-coupling (1972), limited by the reactivity of Grignard reagents.","mc":["The Grignard reagent reduces NiCl₂(dppp) to Ni(0), which adds oxidatively to PhBr.","The methyl group transmetallates from magnesium to nickel.","Reductive elimination gives toluene and regenerates Ni(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kumada coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kumada_coupling#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kumada coupling"},{"x":"Chan–Lam coupling"},{"x":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination"},{"x":"Ullmann reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[592,615],"cond":["NiCl₂(dppp) (cat.), Et₂O"],"p":616},"ex":"Kumada coupling: Ni- or Pd-catalysed coupling of a Grignard reagent with an aryl or vinyl halide. The first catalytic cross-coupling (1972), limited by the reactivity of Grignard reagents.","mc":["The Grignard reagent reduces NiCl₂(dppp) to Ni(0), which adds oxidatively to PhBr.","The methyl group transmetallates from magnesium to nickel.","Reductive elimination gives toluene and regenerates Ni(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kumada coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kumada_coupling#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":617},{"m":618},{"m":619},{"m":620}],"t":"Hiyama coupling","fig":{"r":[598,621],"cond":["Pd(OAc)₂ (cat.), TBAF, THF"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd-catalysed coupling of an organosilane, activated by fluoride, with an organic halide. Fluoride makes a pentacoordinate silicate that can transmetallate.","mc":["Fluoride adds to silicon, forming a pentacoordinate silicate that can transfer its aryl group.","Pd(0) adds oxidatively to PhI, and the phenyl group transmetallates from the silicate to palladium.","Reductive elimination gives biphenyl and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hiyama coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiyama_coupling#Hiyama%E2%80%93Denmark_coupling_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hiyama coupling"},{"x":"Hiyama–Denmark coupling"},{"x":"Negishi coupling"},{"x":"Stille coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[598,621],"cond":["Pd(OAc)₂ (cat.), TBAF, THF"],"p":622},"ex":"Hiyama coupling: Pd-catalysed coupling of an organosilane, activated by fluoride, with an organic halide. Fluoride makes a pentacoordinate silicate that can transmetallate.","mc":["Fluoride adds to silicon, forming a pentacoordinate silicate that can transfer its aryl group.","Pd(0) adds oxidatively to PhI, and the phenyl group transmetallates from the silicate to palladium.","Reductive elimination gives biphenyl and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hiyama coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiyama_coupling#Hiyama%E2%80%93Denmark_coupling_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":623},{"m":624},{"m":625},{"m":626}],"t":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination","fig":{"r":[627,628],"cond":["Pd₂(dba)₃, BINAP (cat.), NaOt-Bu, toluene, 100 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd-catalysed coupling of an amine with an aryl halide to form an aryl C–N bond. Bulky phosphine ligands make the C–N reductive elimination possible.","mc":["Oxidative addition of the aryl bromide to Pd(0)–BINAP.","Morpholine binds palladium and NaOt-Bu removes its N–H proton, giving an aryl–Pd–amido complex.","Reductive elimination forms the C–N bond and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Buchwald–Hartwig amination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buchwald%E2%80%93Hartwig_amination#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination"},{"x":"Rosenmund–von Braun reaction"},{"x":"Miyaura borylation"},{"x":"Heck reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[627,628],"cond":["Pd₂(dba)₃, BINAP (cat.), NaOt-Bu, toluene, 100 °C"],"p":629},"ex":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination: Pd-catalysed coupling of an amine with an aryl halide to form an aryl C–N bond. Bulky phosphine ligands make the C–N reductive elimination possible.","mc":["Oxidative addition of the aryl bromide to Pd(0)–BINAP.","Morpholine binds palladium and NaOt-Bu removes its N–H proton, giving an aryl–Pd–amido complex.","Reductive elimination forms the C–N bond and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Buchwald–Hartwig amination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buchwald%E2%80%93Hartwig_amination#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":630},{"m":631},{"m":632},{"m":633}],"t":"Chan–Lam coupling","fig":{"r":[586,634],"cond":["Cu(OAc)₂, pyridine, CH₂Cl₂, air, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Cu-mediated coupling of an aryl boronic acid with an N–H or O–H compound, in air at room temperature. The oxidative counterpart of the Buchwald–Hartwig reaction.","mc":["The aryl group transmetallates from boron to Cu(II).","Imidazole binds copper (deprotonated by pyridine), and oxidation by O₂ gives a Cu(III) species.","Reductive elimination forms the C–N bond; air reoxidises the copper."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Chan–Lam coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chan%E2%80%93Lam_coupling#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Chan–Lam coupling"},{"x":"Stille coupling"},{"x":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination"},{"x":"Ullmann condensation"}],"fig":{"r":[586,634],"cond":["Cu(OAc)₂, pyridine, CH₂Cl₂, air, rt"],"p":635},"ex":"Chan–Lam coupling: Cu-mediated coupling of an aryl boronic acid with an N–H or O–H compound, in air at room temperature. The oxidative counterpart of the Buchwald–Hartwig reaction.","mc":["The aryl group transmetallates from boron to Cu(II).","Imidazole binds copper (deprotonated by pyridine), and oxidation by O₂ gives a Cu(III) species.","Reductive elimination forms the C–N bond; air reoxidises the copper."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Chan–Lam coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chan%E2%80%93Lam_coupling#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":636},{"m":607},{"m":637},{"m":638}],"t":"Ullmann condensation","fig":{"r":[592,639],"cond":["CuI (cat.), Cs₂CO₃, DMF, 120 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Cu-promoted coupling of an aryl halide with a phenol, amine or thiol. Classically needed copper bronze above 200 °C; ligands now allow milder conditions.","mc":["Cs₂CO₃ deprotonates phenol, and the phenoxide binds Cu(I).","The copper phenoxide adds oxidatively to the aryl bromide, giving a Cu(III) species.","Reductive elimination forms the diaryl ether and regenerates Cu(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ullmann condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ullmann_condensation#Mechanism_of_Ullmann-type_reactions"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ullmann condensation"},{"x":"Miyaura borylation"},{"x":"Kumada coupling"},{"x":"Hiyama–Denmark coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[592,639],"cond":["CuI (cat.), Cs₂CO₃, DMF, 120 °C"],"p":640},"ex":"Ullmann condensation: Cu-promoted coupling of an aryl halide with a phenol, amine or thiol. Classically needed copper bronze above 200 °C; ligands now allow milder conditions.","mc":["Cs₂CO₃ deprotonates phenol, and the phenoxide binds Cu(I).","The copper phenoxide adds oxidatively to the aryl bromide, giving a Cu(III) species.","Reductive elimination forms the diaryl ether and regenerates Cu(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ullmann condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ullmann_condensation#Mechanism_of_Ullmann-type_reactions"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":641},{"m":642},{"m":643},{"m":644}],"t":"Tsuji–Trost reaction","fig":{"r":[645,256],"cond":["Pd(PPh₃)₄ (cat.), NaH, THF"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd-catalysed substitution of an allylic leaving group by a soft nucleophile via a π-allyl complex. With chiral ligands it is the Trost asymmetric allylic alkylation.","mc":["Pd(0) binds the alkene and ionises the allylic acetate, giving a cationic π-allyl Pd(II) complex.","The malonate anion attacks one end of the allyl group, on the face opposite palladium.","The allylated malonate is released and Pd(0) is regenerated."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tsuji–Trost reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsuji%E2%80%93Trost_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Tsuji–Trost reaction"},{"x":"Suzuki–Miyaura coupling"},{"x":"Stille coupling"},{"x":"Ullmann reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[645,256],"cond":["Pd(PPh₃)₄ (cat.), NaH, THF"],"p":646},"ex":"Tsuji–Trost reaction: Pd-catalysed substitution of an allylic leaving group by a soft nucleophile via a π-allyl complex. With chiral ligands it is the Trost asymmetric allylic alkylation.","mc":["Pd(0) binds the alkene and ionises the allylic acetate, giving a cationic π-allyl Pd(II) complex.","The malonate anion attacks one end of the allyl group, on the face opposite palladium.","The allylated malonate is released and Pd(0) is regenerated."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tsuji–Trost reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsuji%E2%80%93Trost_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":647},{"m":583},{"m":648},{"m":584}],"t":"Miyaura borylation","fig":{"r":[585],"cond":["B₂pin₂, Pd(dppf)Cl₂ (cat.), KOAc, dioxane, 80 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd-catalysed conversion of an aryl halide into an aryl boronic ester with B₂pin₂. The weak base KOAc stops the product from undergoing a Suzuki coupling.","mc":["Oxidative addition of the aryl bromide to Pd(0).","Acetate replaces bromide on palladium; the Pd–OAc bond activates the complex for transmetallation with B₂pin₂.","Reductive elimination gives the aryl Bpin and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Miyaura borylation","https://en.wikip